INSERT INTO `tool_content` (`tool_id`, `introduction`, `how_to_use`, `formula`, `formula_explanation`, `calculation_method`, `benefits`, `common_mistakes`, `tips`, `notes`, `disclaimer`) VALUES (1,'The Salary Calculator estimates your take-home pay by subtracting tax and other deductions from your gross salary. It is useful for employees comparing job offers and for employers estimating payroll costs.','Enter your gross salary for the period.\nEnter your effective tax rate as a percentage.\nEnter any other fixed deductions (insurance, retirement contributions, etc.).\nClick Calculate to see your estimated net salary.','Net Salary = Gross Salary − (Gross Salary × Tax Rate) − Other Deductions','Tax is applied as a flat percentage of gross salary in this simplified model; for tiered/progressive tax brackets, use your local tax authority\'s effective rate as the input.',NULL,'Quickly compare job offers or pay changes without building a spreadsheet.',NULL,'Use your effective (average) tax rate, not your marginal bracket rate, for the most accurate estimate.',NULL,''),
(2,'The Overtime Calculator figures out how much extra pay you\'ve earned for hours worked beyond your normal schedule, using a standard overtime multiplier.','Enter your regular hourly rate.\nEnter the number of overtime hours worked.\nSelect the overtime multiplier that applies (time-and-a-half, double time, etc.).\nClick Calculate to see your overtime pay.','Overtime Pay = Hourly Rate × Overtime Multiplier × Overtime Hours','The multiplier reflects your employer\'s or jurisdiction\'s overtime policy — 1.5x (\"time and a half\") is the most common in the US under the FLSA.',NULL,'Verify your paycheck matches what you actually worked.',NULL,'Check your local labor law — some regions require daily overtime after 8 hours even within a standard work week.',NULL,''),
(3,'The Working Hours Calculator finds the total time worked during a shift, automatically handling overnight shifts and subtracting unpaid break time.','Enter your shift start time.\nEnter your shift end time.\nEnter any unpaid break minutes.\nClick Calculate to see total hours worked.','Hours Worked = (End Time − Start Time) − Unpaid Break Minutes','If the end time is earlier than the start time, the tool assumes an overnight shift and adds 24 hours automatically.',NULL,'Quickly fill in timesheets without manual clock-math.',NULL,'Only subtract unpaid breaks — paid breaks should remain part of total hours worked.',NULL,''),
(4,'The Business Days Calculator counts weekdays between two dates, excluding Saturdays and Sundays — useful for estimating delivery times, project deadlines, or notice periods.','Select a start date.\nSelect an end date.\nClick Calculate to see the number of business days between them.','Business Days = Total Days Between Dates − Weekend Days','This tool counts Monday through Friday only; it does not account for public holidays, which vary by country and region.',NULL,'Estimate realistic delivery or project timelines without counting weekends by hand.',NULL,'For legally binding deadlines, confirm public holidays separately since they are not excluded automatically.',NULL,''),
(5,'Economic Order Quantity (EOQ) is the order size that minimizes the combined cost of ordering and holding inventory. It\'s a foundational model in inventory management.','Enter your annual demand in units.\nEnter the fixed cost of placing one order.\nEnter the annual holding cost per unit.\nClick Calculate to see the optimal order quantity.','EOQ = √( (2 × Annual Demand × Ordering Cost) ÷ Holding Cost per Unit )','The formula balances two opposing costs: ordering more often (lower holding cost, higher ordering cost) versus ordering less often (the reverse) — EOQ is the point where total cost is lowest.',NULL,'Reduce total inventory cost by avoiding both over-ordering and excessive reordering.',NULL,'EOQ assumes constant demand and lead time — pair it with a Safety Stock calculation if demand is variable.',NULL,''),
(6,'The Reorder Point (ROP) is the inventory level that triggers a new purchase order, timed so stock doesn\'t run out before the new order arrives.','Enter your average daily usage in units.\nEnter your average supplier lead time in days.\nEnter your safety stock buffer (or 0 if none).\nClick Calculate to find your reorder point.','Reorder Point = (Average Daily Usage × Lead Time) + Safety Stock','Without a safety stock buffer, this represents the exact usage expected during the lead time — any variability in demand or supplier delay could cause a stockout.',NULL,'Avoid stockouts without carrying excess inventory.',NULL,'Use the Safety Stock Calculator first if usage or lead time varies significantly.',NULL,''),
(7,'Safety stock is the extra inventory held to protect against unexpected spikes in demand or delays in supplier lead time, reducing the risk of stockouts.','Enter your maximum daily usage during a busy period.\nEnter your maximum expected supplier lead time.\nEnter your average daily usage.\nEnter your average supplier lead time.\nClick Calculate.','Safety Stock = (Max Daily Usage × Max Lead Time) − (Average Daily Usage × Average Lead Time)','This \"maximum minus average\" method builds a buffer sized to your worst reasonably-expected case, not a guess.',NULL,'Reduce stockout risk without guessing at a buffer size.',NULL,'Feed the result directly into the Reorder Point Calculator\'s safety stock field.',NULL,''),
(8,'Inventory turnover measures how efficiently a business sells through its stock. A higher ratio generally means faster-moving inventory and less capital tied up in stock.','Enter your annual Cost of Goods Sold (COGS).\nEnter your average inventory value for the period.\nClick Calculate to see your turnover ratio and days of inventory on hand.','Inventory Turnover = Cost of Goods Sold ÷ Average Inventory','Dividing 365 by the turnover ratio gives the average number of days inventory sits before being sold — a useful secondary metric.',NULL,'Benchmark inventory efficiency against industry norms or prior periods.',NULL,'A very high turnover isn\'t always good — it can signal insufficient stock levels and lost sales from stockouts.',NULL,''),
(9,'An Equated Monthly Installment (EMI) is the fixed monthly payment on a loan that combines principal and interest, calculated so the loan is fully repaid over its term.','Enter the loan amount (principal).\nEnter the annual interest rate.\nEnter the loan tenure in months.\nClick Calculate to see your EMI, total interest and total payment.','EMI = [P × R × (1+R)^N] ÷ [(1+R)^N − 1], where R = monthly interest rate, N = number of months','This is the standard reducing-balance amortization formula used by nearly all banks for mortgages, auto loans and personal loans.',NULL,'Compare loan offers with different rates or terms before committing.',NULL,'A longer tenure lowers your monthly EMI but increases total interest paid over the life of the loan.',NULL,''),
(10,'Compound interest is interest calculated on both the initial principal and the accumulated interest from previous periods — the mechanism behind long-term investment growth.','Enter your initial principal.\nEnter the annual interest rate.\nSelect how often interest compounds.\nEnter the number of years.\nClick Calculate to see the future value.','A = P × (1 + r/n)^(n×t)','P is principal, r is the annual rate (decimal), n is compounding frequency per year, and t is time in years.',NULL,'See the real impact of compounding frequency and time horizon on investment growth.',NULL,'More frequent compounding (daily vs. annually) yields modestly higher returns at the same nominal rate.',NULL,''),
(11,'Simple interest is calculated only on the original principal, without compounding — common in short-term loans and some fixed-deposit products.','Enter the principal amount.\nEnter the annual interest rate.\nEnter the time period in years.\nClick Calculate to see interest and total payable.','Simple Interest = (Principal × Rate × Time) ÷ 100','Unlike compound interest, the interest amount stays constant each period since it is always calculated on the original principal.',NULL,'Quickly estimate interest on short-term loans, bonds or simple savings products.',NULL,'For anything longer than a year with reinvested interest, use the Compound Interest Calculator instead for an accurate figure.',NULL,''),
(12,'Profit margin shows what percentage of revenue is actual profit after costs, while markup shows profit as a percentage of cost — the two are often confused but answer different questions.','Enter your total revenue.\nEnter your cost of goods sold.\nClick Calculate to see profit, margin and markup.','Profit Margin = (Revenue − Cost) ÷ Revenue × 100','Margin is profit as a % of the selling price (revenue); markup is profit as a % of cost — a 50% margin corresponds to a 100% markup.',NULL,'Set prices correctly and avoid the common margin-vs-markup confusion.',NULL,'Never assume margin and markup percentages are interchangeable — always double-check which one a report is quoting.',NULL,''),
(13,'The break-even point is the sales volume at which total revenue equals total costs — beyond it, every additional unit sold contributes to profit.','Enter your total fixed costs for the period.\nEnter your selling price per unit.\nEnter your variable cost per unit.\nClick Calculate to see your break-even point.','Break-Even Units = Fixed Costs ÷ (Selling Price − Variable Cost per Unit)','The denominator is called the \"contribution margin\" — how much each unit sold contributes toward covering fixed costs after variable costs are paid.',NULL,'Set realistic sales targets and pricing before launching a product.',NULL,'If your variable cost is close to or above your selling price, no volume of sales will break even — you\'ll need to raise price or cut costs.',NULL,''),
(14,'The current ratio is a liquidity ratio that measures whether a company has enough current assets to cover its current liabilities.','Enter total current assets.\nEnter total current liabilities.\nClick Calculate to see the current ratio and a liquidity note.','Current Ratio = Current Assets ÷ Current Liabilities','A ratio above 1 means current assets exceed current liabilities; ratios between 1.5 and 3 are generally considered healthy, though this varies by industry.',NULL,'Quickly assess short-term financial health during an audit or review.',NULL,'Compare against industry peers rather than a single universal benchmark — capital-intensive industries often run lower ratios.',NULL,''),
(15,'Also called the acid-test ratio, the quick ratio measures a company\'s ability to meet short-term obligations using only its most liquid assets — excluding inventory, which can be slow to convert to cash.','Enter total current assets.\nEnter total inventory value.\nEnter total current liabilities.\nClick Calculate to see the quick ratio.','Quick Ratio = (Current Assets − Inventory) ÷ Current Liabilities','Excluding inventory gives a more conservative view of liquidity than the current ratio, since inventory isn\'t always quickly convertible to cash at full value.',NULL,'Get a stricter, more conservative liquidity check than the current ratio alone.',NULL,'A quick ratio near or above 1 is generally considered strong; well below 1 may warrant a closer look at cash flow.',NULL,''),
(16,'The debt-to-equity (D/E) ratio compares total liabilities to shareholders\' equity, indicating how leveraged a company is and how much financial risk it carries.','Enter total liabilities.\nEnter total shareholders\' equity.\nClick Calculate to see the debt-to-equity ratio.','D/E Ratio = Total Liabilities ÷ Total Shareholders\' Equity','A ratio above 1 means the company has more debt than equity financing its assets — higher leverage can mean higher risk but also potentially higher returns on equity.',NULL,'Assess financial leverage and risk quickly during a review or audit.',NULL,'Compare against industry peers — capital-intensive sectors like utilities and manufacturing typically run higher D/E ratios than software companies.',NULL,''),
(17,'EBITDA is a measure of a company\'s core operating profitability, excluding the effects of financing decisions, tax environments and non-cash accounting charges.','Enter net income.\nEnter interest expense.\nEnter taxes paid.\nEnter depreciation and amortization.\nClick Calculate to see EBITDA.','EBITDA = Net Income + Interest + Taxes + Depreciation + Amortization','By adding back interest, taxes, and non-cash D&A charges to net income, EBITDA approximates cash operating profit, making it useful for comparing companies with different capital structures.',NULL,'Compare operating performance across companies regardless of financing or tax structure.',NULL,'EBITDA is not a substitute for net income or cash flow — always review it alongside full financial statements.',NULL,''),
(18,'This tool formats JSON with proper indentation for readability, validates its syntax, and can minify it back down for production use — all processed locally in your browser.','Paste your JSON into the text box.\nClick Format & Validate to pretty-print and check for errors.\nClick Minify to compress it back to a single line.','N/A — this is a formatting/validation tool, not a calculation.','The tool uses the browser\'s native JSON.parse/JSON.stringify, so any error message reflects an actual JSON syntax problem in your input.',NULL,'Catch JSON syntax errors instantly without leaving your browser or sending data to a server.',NULL,'Trailing commas and single quotes are common causes of \"invalid JSON\" errors — JSON requires double quotes and no trailing commas.',NULL,''),
(19,'Base64 encoding converts binary or text data into an ASCII string format, commonly used to embed data in URLs, JSON payloads or email attachments.','Type or paste your text.\nClick Encode to Base64, or paste a Base64 string and click Decode from Base64.','N/A — this is an encoding/decoding tool, not a calculation.','Base64 represents binary data using 64 printable ASCII characters (A-Z, a-z, 0-9, +, /), increasing size by roughly 33%.',NULL,'Quickly debug API payloads, tokens or embedded data without writing code.',NULL,'Base64 is encoding, not encryption — never use it alone to protect sensitive data.',NULL,''),
(20,'This tool generates cryptographically random passwords using your browser\'s Web Crypto API, letting you control length and which character types are included.','Set your desired password length.\nCheck the character types to include (uppercase, lowercase, numbers, symbols).\nClick Generate Password.','N/A — this is a random generation tool, not a calculation.','Passwords are generated using crypto.getRandomValues(), a cryptographically secure random number source built into modern browsers — not Math.random(), which is not secure for this purpose.',NULL,'Create strong, unique passwords for every account without reusing weak ones.',NULL,'Aim for at least 16 characters with all four character types enabled for strong general-purpose passwords.',NULL,''),
(21,'A UUID (Universally Unique Identifier) is a 128-bit value used to uniquely identify records, sessions or resources without needing a central authority.','Choose how many UUIDs you need.\nClick Generate UUID (v4) to create them.','N/A — this is a random generation tool, not a calculation.','Version 4 UUIDs are generated from random or pseudo-random numbers, with a few fixed bits marking the version and variant per the UUID specification.',NULL,'Generate unique IDs for database records, API keys or test data instantly.',NULL,'UUIDs are not sequential — if you need sortable IDs, consider a ULID or a database auto-increment column instead.',NULL,''),
(22,'A Unix timestamp counts the seconds elapsed since January 1, 1970 (UTC) — the \"Unix epoch\" — and is used throughout programming for representing dates.','Enter a Unix timestamp and click Convert to Date, or\nPick a date and time and click Convert to Timestamp.','Timestamp = Seconds Elapsed Since 1970-01-01T00:00:00 UTC','This tool displays converted dates in UTC to avoid ambiguity — convert to your local timezone separately if needed.',NULL,'Debug API responses, log files and databases that store dates as Unix timestamps.',NULL,'Some systems (like JavaScript\'s Date.now()) use milliseconds, not seconds — divide by 1000 first if your timestamp looks 1,000x too large.',NULL,''),
(23,'This tool simplifies image dimensions to their aspect ratio, and can scale a new width or height proportionally to match the original ratio.','Enter the original width and height in pixels.\nOptionally enter a new width OR new height to scale proportionally.\nClick Calculate.','Aspect Ratio = Width ÷ GCD(Width, Height) : Height ÷ GCD(Width, Height)','GCD is the greatest common divisor — dividing both dimensions by it reduces the ratio to its simplest whole-number form (e.g. 1920×1080 simplifies to 16:9).',NULL,'Resize images without distortion by keeping the original proportions.',NULL,'Leave the new width or new height blank (not both) to have this tool calculate the matching dimension for you.',NULL,''),
(24,'Different design and print tools expect colors in different formats — this tool converts a single HEX color into RGB, HSL and CMYK simultaneously.','Enter a HEX color code, or pick one with the color swatch.\nClick Convert Color to see it in RGB, HSL and CMYK.','RGB is derived from HEX pairs; HSL and CMYK are derived mathematically from the RGB values.','CMYK conversion from RGB is an approximation (screen colors and print inks don\'t map perfectly) — always confirm final print colors with your printer\'s color profile.',NULL,'Move a single brand color across web (HEX/RGB/HSL) and print (CMYK) workflows without manual conversion.',NULL,'CMYK values shown are a standard approximation — professional print jobs should use a calibrated ICC color profile.',NULL,''),
(25,'DPI (dots per inch) determines how sharp an image looks when printed at a specific physical size — the same image can look great on screen but blurry in print if the DPI is too low.','Enter the image width in pixels.\nEnter your intended print width in inches.\nClick Calculate DPI.','DPI = Image Width in Pixels ÷ Print Width in Inches','Professional print generally requires 300 DPI or higher; large-format prints viewed from a distance (banners, posters) can use much lower DPI.',NULL,'Avoid pixelated prints by checking resolution before sending a file to print.',NULL,'For large banners viewed from several feet away, 100-150 DPI is often perfectly acceptable — 300 DPI isn\'t always necessary.',NULL,''),
(26,'Every social platform has its own recommended image dimensions for posts, covers and stories — this tool provides a quick lookup so you never have to search for them again.','Select the platform and content type you\'re designing for.\nClick Get Recommended Size to see the exact pixel dimensions.','N/A — this is a reference lookup, not a calculation.','Dimensions reflect each platform\'s current published guidelines at time of writing; platforms occasionally update these specifications.',NULL,'Design assets at the correct size the first time, avoiding platform-side cropping or compression.',NULL,'Platforms periodically change their recommended sizes — double check against the platform\'s official help center for critical campaigns.',NULL,''),
(27,'Estimating video file size ahead of export or upload helps plan storage and bandwidth needs, especially for long-form content or high-bitrate footage.','Enter the video bitrate in Mbps.\nEnter the duration in minutes.\nEnter the audio bitrate in kbps (default 192).\nClick Calculate File Size.','File Size (MB) = ((Video Bitrate in bits + Audio Bitrate in bits) × Duration in seconds) ÷ 8 ÷ 1,048,576','Bitrate measures data per second; multiplying by total duration and converting bits to bytes (÷8) and then to megabytes gives the final file size.',NULL,'Plan storage and upload time before exporting a long video render.',NULL,'Actual file size can vary slightly from this estimate depending on codec efficiency and variable bitrate encoding.',NULL,''),
(28,'When you need to fit a video under a specific file size limit (e.g. for an upload cap or storage budget), this tool works backward to find the required bitrate.','Enter your target file size in MB.\nEnter the video duration in minutes.\nEnter the audio bitrate in kbps.\nClick Calculate Required Bitrate.','Video Bitrate (kbps) = (Target Size in bits ÷ Duration in seconds ÷ 1000) − Audio Bitrate','This rearranges the file-size formula to solve for bitrate instead, after reserving space for the audio track.',NULL,'Hit exact file size limits for platforms with upload caps.',NULL,'Leave a small safety margin below your hard limit, since real-world encoding can slightly overshoot a target bitrate.',NULL,''),
(29,'Editors often need to know exact frame counts for precise trimming, animation timing, or matching frame-accurate cuts across clips.','Enter the video duration in seconds.\nSelect the frame rate (FPS).\nClick Calculate Total Frames.','Total Frames = Duration in Seconds × Frames per Second','This is a direct multiplication — frame rate defines how many still images make up one second of video.',NULL,'Plan frame-accurate edits, animations or export settings.',NULL,'24fps is standard for cinematic footage, 30fps for typical online video, and 60fps+ for smooth motion or slow-motion source footage.',NULL,''),
(30,'This tool gives a rough estimate of ad revenue based on view count and RPM (revenue per 1,000 views) — a widely used, if approximate, benchmark for creator income planning.','Enter your monthly (or total) views.\nEnter your estimated RPM based on your niche and audience geography.\nClick Estimate Revenue.','Estimated Revenue = (Views ÷ 1,000) × RPM','RPM varies enormously by niche, audience country, seasonality and ad demand — this is a planning estimate, not a guaranteed figure.',NULL,'Rough-plan content strategy and revenue expectations before committing to a niche.',NULL,'Check your own YouTube Studio Analytics for your actual historical RPM rather than guessing — it varies widely by channel.',NULL,''),
(31,'GPA (Grade Point Average) is a weighted average of your grades across courses, weighted by each course\'s credit hours — the standard measure of academic performance in the US.','Enter the credit hours for each course.\nSelect the letter grade earned in each course.\nAdd rows for additional courses as needed.\nClick Calculate GPA.','GPA = Σ(Credit Hours × Grade Points) ÷ Σ(Credit Hours)','Each letter grade maps to a grade point value (A=4.0, B=3.0, etc.) on a standard 4.0 scale; multiplying by credit hours weights heavier courses more.',NULL,'Track academic standing across a semester or full academic career.',NULL,'Some schools use slight variations of the 4.0 scale (e.g. A- = 3.7) — check with your registrar if your school\'s scale differs.',NULL,''),
(32,'Convert raw marks obtained out of a total into a percentage score and an approximate letter grade.','Enter the marks you obtained.\nEnter the total possible marks.\nClick Calculate Grade.','Percentage = (Marks Obtained ÷ Total Marks) × 100','The letter grade shown uses a common US grading scale (A=90%+, B=80-89%, etc.) — your institution\'s actual scale may differ.',NULL,'Quickly check exam or assignment results without manual division.',NULL,'Check your syllabus for your instructor\'s exact grading scale, since cutoffs vary between schools and courses.',NULL,''),
(33,'Many courses require a minimum attendance percentage to remain eligible for exams — this tool tracks your current standing and tells you exactly how much room you have.','Enter the number of classes you\'ve attended.\nEnter the total number of classes held so far.\nEnter your required attendance percentage.\nClick Calculate Attendance.','Attendance % = (Classes Attended ÷ Total Classes) × 100','If you\'re above the requirement, the tool estimates how many more classes you can miss and stay compliant; if below, it estimates how many consecutive classes you need to attend to recover.',NULL,'Avoid last-minute surprises about exam eligibility due to low attendance.',NULL,'Recalculate regularly through the term, since the \"classes you can miss\" estimate assumes no further classes are held beyond the total you enter.',NULL,''),
(34,'The Pomodoro Technique breaks work into focused intervals (traditionally 25 minutes) separated by short breaks, helping maintain concentration and avoid burnout during study sessions.','Set your preferred focus duration and break duration.\nClick Start to begin the focus session.\nWhen the timer reaches zero it automatically switches to a break, and back again.','N/A — this is a countdown timer, not a calculation.','The classic Pomodoro cycle is 25 minutes focus / 5 minutes break, though many students adjust these durations to fit their own attention span.',NULL,'Improve focus and reduce procrastination during study or work sessions.',NULL,'Keep your phone out of reach during the focus interval — the technique works best with zero interruptions.',NULL,''),
(35,'This tool calculates how much concrete you need for a slab based on its length, width and thickness — essential for ordering the right amount of ready-mix concrete.','Enter the slab length in feet.\nEnter the slab width in feet.\nEnter the slab thickness in inches.\nClick Calculate Concrete Needed.','Volume (cu yd) = (Length × Width × Thickness in feet) ÷ 27','Thickness is converted from inches to feet (÷12) before multiplying, since concrete is typically ordered in cubic yards (27 cubic feet = 1 cubic yard).',NULL,'Order the right amount of ready-mix concrete and avoid costly under- or over-ordering.',NULL,'Always order 5-10% extra to account for spillage, uneven subgrade and formwork variance.',NULL,''),
(36,'Estimate the amount of paint required for a project based on wall area, number of coats, and your paint\'s coverage rate.','Enter the total wall area in square feet.\nEnter the number of coats you plan to apply.\nEnter your paint\'s coverage rate per gallon (check the can label).\nClick Calculate Paint Needed.','Gallons Needed = (Wall Area × Number of Coats) ÷ Coverage per Gallon','Coverage rate varies by paint brand and surface texture — always check the actual coverage listed on your specific paint can.',NULL,'Buy the right amount of paint in one trip instead of guessing and running short mid-project.',NULL,'Textured or porous surfaces (like new drywall or brick) absorb more paint — round up your coverage estimate for those surfaces.',NULL,''),
(37,'This tool estimates how many tiles you need to cover a room, accounting for a standard cut-and-waste allowance.','Enter your room\'s total area in square feet.\nEnter the area covered by a single tile.\nEnter a waste/cut allowance percentage.\nClick Calculate Tiles Needed.','Tiles Needed = (Room Area ÷ Tile Area) × (1 + Waste Allowance %)','The waste allowance accounts for cut pieces at edges, breakage, and pattern-matching loss (diagonal layouts need a higher allowance than straight layouts).',NULL,'Order enough tile in one purchase, including realistic overage for cuts and breakage.',NULL,'Diagonal tile layouts typically need a 15-20% waste allowance rather than the standard 10%, due to more angled cuts.',NULL,''),
(38,'Estimate the total number of standard modular bricks needed to build a wall of a given area, including a waste allowance for cuts and breakage.','Enter the total wall area in square feet.\nConfirm or adjust bricks-per-square-foot for your brick size (standard modular ≈ 6.86).\nEnter a waste allowance percentage.\nClick Calculate Bricks Needed.','Bricks Needed = Wall Area × Bricks per Sq Ft × (1 + Waste %)','Bricks-per-square-foot depends on brick size and mortar joint thickness — the default (6.86) reflects standard US modular brick with a 3/8\" mortar joint.',NULL,'Order the right quantity of brick for a project without expensive last-minute reorders.',NULL,'Confirm your specific brick dimensions with your supplier — non-standard or imported brick sizes will need a different bricks-per-sq-ft value.',NULL,''),
(39,'A roof\'s actual surface area is larger than the building\'s footprint because of its slope (pitch) — this tool applies a standard pitch multiplier to estimate true roof area.','Enter the building footprint area in square feet.\nSelect the roof pitch (rise per 12\" of run).\nClick Calculate Roof Area.','Roof Area = Building Footprint × Pitch Multiplier','The pitch multiplier accounts for the extra surface area created by the roof\'s slope — a steeper pitch (e.g. 12/12) has a much higher multiplier than a shallow one (e.g. 3/12).',NULL,'Estimate roofing material needs (shingles, underlayment) before requesting contractor quotes.',NULL,'Roofing materials are usually sold by the \"square\" (100 sq ft) — divide your result by 100 to get the number of squares needed.',NULL,''),
(40,'Board feet is the standard unit for measuring and pricing rough lumber in the US, representing the volume of a board 1 inch thick, 12 inches wide, and 12 inches long.','Enter the board thickness in inches.\nEnter the board width in inches.\nEnter the board length in feet.\nEnter how many boards you need.\nClick Calculate Board Feet.','Board Feet = (Thickness in in. × Width in in. × Length in ft.) ÷ 12','This formula converts the board\'s volume into the standard board-foot unit used by lumber yards for pricing rough-sawn hardwood and softwood.',NULL,'Get accurate lumber quantities and costs when ordering from a lumber yard that prices by the board foot.',NULL,'Nominal lumber dimensions (like a \"2x4\") are usually larger than actual dressed dimensions — use actual measured dimensions for precise board footage.',NULL,''),
(41,'Estimate the number of standard 4×8 plywood sheets (or any custom sheet size) needed to cover a given project area, including a cutting waste allowance.','Enter your total project area in square feet.\nEnter the sheet size in square feet (standard 4x8 = 32).\nEnter a waste allowance percentage.\nClick Calculate Sheets Needed.','Sheets Needed = (Project Area ÷ Sheet Size) × (1 + Waste %)','The waste allowance accounts for off-cuts, irregular shapes, and pieces too small to reuse from a cut sheet.',NULL,'Buy the right number of sheets in one trip to the lumber yard.',NULL,'Complex cuts (curves, many small pieces) warrant a higher waste allowance (15-20%) than simple rectangular panels (5-10%).',NULL,''),
(42,'When building a picture frame, planter box, or any multi-sided frame, each joint needs a precise miter angle — this tool calculates it for any regular polygon shape.','Enter the number of sides your frame will have (4 for a standard rectangle frame, 6 for hexagon, 8 for octagon, etc.).\nClick Calculate Miter Angle.','Miter Angle = 180° ÷ Number of Sides','This is derived from the interior angle of a regular polygon; each corner is split evenly between the two adjoining pieces.',NULL,'Cut accurate joints on the first try for frames, planters or multi-sided projects.',NULL,'Set your miter saw to the calculated angle from square (0°), not from parallel — most saws are calibrated this way by default.',NULL,''),
(43,'This tool calculates a safe, comfortable stair layout — determining the number of steps and exact riser height from the total floor-to-floor rise.','Enter the total rise (floor-to-floor height) in inches.\nEnter your target riser height (typically 7-7.75 inches).\nEnter your desired tread depth (typically 10-11 inches).\nClick Calculate Stair Layout.','Number of Steps = Round(Total Rise ÷ Target Riser Height); Actual Riser = Total Rise ÷ Number of Steps','Rounding to a whole number of steps means the actual riser height will differ slightly from your target — all risers in a staircase should be equal for safety, so this adjusted figure is what you actually cut.',NULL,'Build code-compliant, evenly-spaced stairs without trial and error.',NULL,'Most building codes require riser heights between 7 and 7.75 inches and tread depth of at least 10 inches — check your local code before finalizing.',NULL,''),
(44,'Estimate total fuel expense for a trip or delivery route, useful for budgeting, route planning, and comparing vehicle efficiency.','Enter the trip distance in miles.\nEnter your vehicle\'s fuel efficiency in miles per gallon.\nEnter the current fuel price per gallon.\nClick Calculate Fuel Cost.','Fuel Cost = (Distance ÷ Fuel Efficiency) × Price per Gallon','Distance divided by MPG gives gallons needed; multiplying by price per gallon gives total cost.',NULL,'Budget trips accurately and compare cost-efficiency between vehicles or routes.',NULL,'Use your vehicle\'s real-world average MPG (from your trip computer or manual tracking) rather than the manufacturer\'s rated figure for a more accurate estimate.',NULL,''),
(45,'CBM (Cubic Meters) is the standard unit for measuring shipment volume in international freight, used to calculate container space and volumetric shipping charges.','Enter the box length, width and height in centimeters.\nEnter the quantity of identical boxes.\nClick Calculate CBM.','CBM = (Length × Width × Height in cm) ÷ 1,000,000 × Quantity','Dividing by 1,000,000 converts cubic centimeters to cubic meters; multiplying by quantity gives the total shipment volume.',NULL,'Estimate container or pallet space requirements and compare against carrier volume-based pricing.',NULL,'Always measure the outer (packed) dimensions of your cartons, not the product dimensions alone, for an accurate shipping CBM.',NULL,''),
(46,'Couriers charge shipping based on whichever is higher: a package\'s actual weight, or its \"volumetric weight\" calculated from its dimensions — this protects carriers from bulky, lightweight shipments taking up expensive space cheaply.','Enter the package length, width and height in centimeters.\nEnter your carrier\'s volumetric divisor (often 5000 or 6000 — check your carrier\'s terms).\nEnter the actual weight in kg.\nClick Calculate Chargeable Weight.','Volumetric Weight = (Length × Width × Height in cm) ÷ Divisor; Chargeable Weight = MAX(Volumetric Weight, Actual Weight)','A lower divisor produces a higher volumetric weight — always confirm your specific carrier\'s divisor, as it varies between air freight, courier, and regional services.',NULL,'Predict actual shipping charges before booking, avoiding billing surprises.',NULL,'Different carriers and even different service tiers from the same carrier can use different divisors — always check the specific service\'s published rate card.',NULL,''),
(47,'Estimate the total shipping cost of a freight shipment using its chargeable weight, your carrier\'s per-kilogram rate, and any additional fixed fees.','Enter the shipment\'s chargeable weight in kg (use the Volumetric Weight Calculator first if needed).\nEnter your carrier\'s rate per kg.\nEnter any additional fixed fees (customs, handling, etc.).\nClick Calculate Freight Cost.','Freight Cost = (Chargeable Weight × Rate per kg) + Additional Fees','Chargeable weight is whichever is higher between actual and volumetric weight — see the Volumetric Weight Calculator for that calculation.',NULL,'Budget and compare freight quotes accurately across carriers.',NULL,'Get the chargeable weight right first — using actual weight alone can significantly underestimate cost for bulky, lightweight shipments.',NULL,''),
(48,'BMI (Body Mass Index) is a widely-used screening measure that relates body weight to height. It is a general population screening tool, not a diagnosis of individual health.','Select metric (kg/cm) or imperial (lb/in) units.\nEnter your weight.\nEnter your height.\nClick Calculate BMI.','Metric: BMI = Weight (kg) ÷ Height (m)²  •  Imperial: BMI = (Weight (lb) ÷ Height (in)²) × 703','BMI categories (underweight, normal, overweight, obese) are based on WHO population-level thresholds and do not account for individual factors like muscle mass, bone density or body composition.',NULL,'Quick general health screening indicator to discuss with a healthcare provider.',NULL,'BMI does not distinguish muscle from fat — athletes with high muscle mass may show a high BMI despite low body fat. Consult a healthcare professional for a full assessment.',NULL,''),
(49,'Quickly calculate how much to tip and split a restaurant bill fairly among any number of people.','Enter the bill amount.\nEnter your desired tip percentage.\nEnter the number of people splitting the bill.\nClick Calculate Tip.','Total = Bill + (Bill × Tip %); Per Person = Total ÷ Number of People','Tip is calculated on the pre-tip bill total, then the grand total is divided evenly across all people splitting.',NULL,'Settle group bills fairly and quickly without mental math at the table.',NULL,'Tipping norms vary widely by country — 15-20% is standard in the US, while it is uncommon or already included in many other regions.',NULL,''),
(50,'Calculate a precise age (or the time elapsed between any two dates) in years, months and days — accounting correctly for varying month lengths and leap years.','Enter the date of birth.\nOptionally enter an \'as of\' date (defaults to today).\nClick Calculate Age.','Age = (As-Of Date − Date of Birth), expressed in years, months and days','The calculation correctly borrows days/months when the as-of date\'s day-of-month is earlier than the birth date\'s, accounting for real calendar month lengths.',NULL,'Get an exact age for legal forms, eligibility checks, or curiosity — down to the day.',NULL,'Use the \"as of\" date field to calculate someone\'s age at a specific past or future date, not just today.',NULL,''),
(51,'A flexible, all-in-one converter covering the most commonly needed unit categories: length, weight, temperature, area and volume.','Select a unit category.\nChoose the \'from\' and \'to\' units.\nEnter the value to convert.\nClick Convert.','Value is converted through a common base unit (e.g. meters for length) before converting to the target unit; temperature uses dedicated linear formulas (Celsius/Fahrenheit/Kelvin).','Using a common base unit for each category keeps the conversion table small and avoids needing a direct formula between every possible unit pair.',NULL,'One tool for dozens of everyday conversions instead of searching for each one individually.',NULL,'Temperature conversions are not linear multiples like other units — this tool switches to the correct dedicated formula automatically when you select Temperature.',NULL,''),
(52,'Convert an amount between currencies using exchange rates configured by the site administrator, with a clearly displayed \"last updated\" timestamp for transparency.','Enter the amount to convert.\nSelect the source currency.\nSelect the target currency.\nClick Convert.','Converted Amount = (Amount ÷ Rate of Source Currency) × Rate of Target Currency, relative to the configured base currency','All stored rates are relative to a single base currency; converting between two non-base currencies routes through that base internally.',NULL,'Quick currency conversion for travel, shopping or invoicing.',NULL,'Always check the \"rates last updated\" note — this tool does not claim real-time market rates unless a live API is configured by the site admin.',NULL,''),
(53,'A flexible calculator for the three most common percentage problems: finding X% of a number, finding what percent one number is of another, and calculating percentage change.','Select the calculation type you need.\nEnter the X and Y values.\nClick Calculate.','X% of Y = (X÷100)×Y  •  X is what % of Y = (X÷Y)×100  •  % Change from X to Y = ((Y−X)÷|X|)×100','Percentage change uses the absolute value of the starting number (X) in the denominator so the sign of the result correctly reflects an increase or decrease even when X is negative.',NULL,'Handle the most common everyday percentage questions in one tool instead of three.',NULL,'For \"percentage change\", a negative result means a decrease and a positive result means an increase from X to Y.',NULL,''),
(54,'Find the precise duration between any two dates — useful for project planning, countdowns, or calculating durations for contracts and events.','Select a start date.\nSelect an end date.\nClick Calculate Difference.','Difference = |End Date − Start Date|, expressed in total days plus years/months/days breakdown','The tool automatically handles either date being entered first — order doesn\'t matter, the difference is always shown as a positive duration.',NULL,'Quickly calculate durations for contracts, deadlines, countdowns or anniversaries.',NULL,'Total days shown includes both endpoints\' calendar difference — double check whether your use case needs an inclusive or exclusive day count.',NULL,''),
(55,'Generate a QR code from any text, URL, or short message that can be scanned by any modern smartphone camera — useful for sharing links, contact info, or wifi credentials.','Enter the text or URL you want to encode.\nChoose your desired output size.\nClick Generate QR Code.\nDownload the PNG once it\'s generated.','N/A — this is a code-generation tool, not a calculation.','QR codes encode data using a standardized matrix barcode format readable by any modern smartphone camera or QR scanning app.',NULL,'Create QR codes for business cards, flyers, or product packaging without a design tool.',NULL,'Test your generated QR code with an actual phone camera before printing it at scale, especially for long URLs which create denser codes.',NULL,''),
(56,'A weekly timesheet totals daily hours and flags anything over your overtime threshold, so you can verify a pay period before submitting it.','Enter hours worked for each day of the week.\nSet your weekly overtime threshold.\nClick Calculate Weekly Hours.','Total Hours = Sum of Daily Hours; Overtime = MAX(0, Total − Threshold)','Hours up to the threshold count as regular time; anything beyond it is reported separately as overtime.',NULL,'Catch timesheet errors before payroll processes them.',NULL,'Enter 0 for days not worked rather than leaving them blank.',NULL,''),
(57,'Track how many paid leave days you have left by subtracting days already used and any pending requests from your total accrued balance.','Enter total leave days accrued so far.\nEnter leave days already used.\nEnter any pending/requested leave.\nClick Calculate Leave Balance.','Leave Balance = Accrued − Used − Pending','Including pending requests in the calculation shows your true remaining balance if all pending requests are approved.',NULL,'Plan future time off without overdrawing your leave balance.',NULL,'Check your employer\'s policy on whether unused leave carries over or expires at year end.',NULL,''),
(58,'Find the exact last working day by adding a required notice period (in days) to the date notice was given.','Select the date notice was given.\nEnter the required notice period in days.\nClick Calculate Last Working Day.','Last Working Day = Notice Date + Notice Period (days)','This is a straight calendar-day addition; it does not exclude weekends or holidays unless your contract specifies working days instead of calendar days.',NULL,'Confirm handover and offboarding timelines precisely.',NULL,'Double-check whether your contract specifies calendar days or working days for the notice period.',NULL,''),
(59,'An employee\'s true cost to a business is significantly more than their salary once benefits, payroll taxes and overhead are included — this tool estimates that fully-loaded cost.','Enter the employee\'s annual base salary.\nEnter benefits cost as a % of salary.\nEnter employer payroll tax %.\nEnter any other overhead (equipment, office space).\nClick Calculate.','Total Cost = Salary + (Salary × Benefits %) + (Salary × Payroll Tax %) + Other Overhead','The resulting \"load multiplier\" (total cost ÷ salary) is commonly between 1.25x and 1.4x base salary for typical US employment.',NULL,'Budget hiring decisions using the real cost, not just the offered salary.',NULL,'Benefits and payroll tax percentages vary significantly by country — use your actual local rates.',NULL,''),
(60,'Calculate when a purchase order should arrive by adding supplier lead time (and any safety buffer) to the order date.','Select the order date.\nEnter the supplier\'s typical lead time in days.\nEnter an optional safety buffer.\nClick Calculate.','Expected Arrival = Order Date + Lead Time + Safety Buffer','The safety buffer accounts for typical variability in supplier performance beyond their quoted lead time.',NULL,'Set realistic customer delivery promises and reorder timing.',NULL,'Track actual vs. quoted lead times over several orders to calibrate a realistic buffer per supplier.',NULL,''),
(61,'Estimate how much warehouse volume you need to store a given number of units, accounting for realistic space utilization rather than perfect stacking.','Enter the number of units to store.\nEnter the volume per unit.\nEnter your expected space utilization percentage.\nClick Calculate.','Space Needed = (Units × Volume per Unit) ÷ Utilization %','Utilization accounts for aisles, racking gaps and irregular stacking — 100% utilization is never realistic in a working warehouse.',NULL,'Right-size warehouse leases or storage racking before committing.',NULL,'A utilization rate of 65-80% is typical for palletized warehouse storage with aisles.',NULL,''),
(62,'Figure out how many cartons of a given size fit on a pallet, testing both carton orientations to find the most efficient layout per layer.','Enter your pallet\'s length, width and max stack height.\nEnter the carton\'s length, width and height.\nClick Calculate Cartons per Pallet.','Cartons per Pallet = (Cartons per Layer) × (Layers = Stack Height ÷ Carton Height)','The tool checks both possible carton orientations on the pallet footprint and uses whichever produces more cartons per layer.',NULL,'Plan pallet quantities accurately for shipping and storage.',NULL,'Leave room in max stack height for pallet + stretch wrap clearance under warehouse racking.',NULL,''),
(63,'Estimate how many cartons a shipping container can hold, checking both volume capacity and weight limits since either can be the limiting factor.','Select your container type.\nEnter carton dimensions.\nEnter carton weight.\nClick Calculate Max Cartons.','Max Cartons = MIN(Container Volume × 0.85 ÷ Carton Volume, Container Max Weight ÷ Carton Weight)','The 85% factor reflects realistic loading efficiency — perfect 100% cube utilization is never achievable with irregular cargo and required load stability.',NULL,'Plan container quantities and freight costs before booking a shipment.',NULL,'Always confirm the exact usable dimensions and weight limit with your carrier — they vary slightly by container age and manufacturer.',NULL,''),
(64,'Quickly check whether your desired order quantity meets a supplier\'s Minimum Order Quantity (MOQ), and see the extra cost of ordering up to the MOQ if not.','Enter your desired order quantity.\nEnter the supplier\'s MOQ.\nEnter unit cost (optional, to see extra spend).\nClick Check Against MOQ.','Order Quantity = MAX(Desired Quantity, MOQ)','If desired quantity is below MOQ, you must either order up to the MOQ, negotiate a lower MOQ, or find an alternate supplier.',NULL,'Avoid surprise costs from ordering below a supplier\'s minimum.',NULL,'MOQs are often negotiable, especially for repeat orders or long-term contracts — don\'t assume they\'re fixed.',NULL,''),
(65,'The Compound Annual Growth Rate (CAGR) expresses an investment\'s return as a smoothed annual percentage, making it easy to compare investments held over different time periods.','Enter your initial investment amount.\nEnter the final value.\nEnter the number of years held.\nClick Calculate Annualized Return.','CAGR = ((Final Value ÷ Initial Value)^(1 ÷ Years) − 1) × 100','Unlike simple total return, CAGR accounts for the compounding effect over time, giving a fair year-over-year comparison figure.',NULL,'Compare investments with different holding periods on equal footing.',NULL,'CAGR smooths out volatility — a volatile investment could have wild swings even with a steady CAGR.',NULL,''),
(66,'Return on Investment (ROI) measures the profitability of an investment relative to its cost, expressed as a simple percentage.','Enter the total gain from the investment.\nEnter the total cost of the investment.\nClick Calculate ROI.','ROI = ((Gain − Cost) ÷ Cost) × 100','ROI does not account for the time period over which the gain occurred — use CAGR instead when comparing investments of different durations.',NULL,'Quickly compare the profitability of different investments or projects.',NULL,'ROI alone doesn\'t capture risk or time horizon — pair it with other metrics for a full picture.',NULL,''),
(67,'Quickly calculate a sale price and the dollar amount saved from any percentage discount.','Enter the original price.\nEnter the discount percentage.\nClick Calculate Final Price.','Final Price = Original Price × (1 − Discount % ÷ 100)','The savings amount is simply the original price minus the final price.',NULL,'Verify sale prices and stacked discounts while shopping or pricing products.',NULL,'For stacked discounts (e.g. 20% then an additional 10%), apply them sequentially rather than adding the percentages together.',NULL,''),
(68,'Estimate tax owed and net income using a flat effective tax rate you provide — since actual tax brackets and rules vary enormously by country and region, this tool keeps the rate fully configurable rather than hardcoding any one jurisdiction\'s rules.','Enter your taxable income.\nEnter your effective (average) tax rate.\nClick Calculate Tax Owed.','Tax Owed = Taxable Income × Effective Tax Rate','Your effective rate (total tax ÷ total income) already accounts for progressive brackets, deductions and credits — look it up from a prior tax return or your tax authority\'s tables for the most accurate result.',NULL,'Quickly estimate take-home pay or plan for a tax bill.',NULL,'This is not tax advice — consult a tax professional for filing purposes.',NULL,''),
(69,'Calculate a final invoice total by applying a discount and then tax to a subtotal — matching how most invoicing systems apply tax to the post-discount amount.','Enter the invoice subtotal.\nEnter any discount percentage.\nEnter the applicable tax rate.\nClick Calculate Invoice Total.','Total = (Subtotal × (1 − Discount%)) × (1 + Tax%)','Tax is calculated on the discounted amount, not the original subtotal, matching standard invoicing practice in most jurisdictions.',NULL,'Double-check invoice totals before sending or paying.',NULL,'Confirm with your accountant whether your jurisdiction taxes the pre- or post-discount amount, as this can occasionally differ.',NULL,''),
(70,'Straight-line depreciation spreads an asset\'s cost evenly over its useful life — the simplest and most widely used depreciation method for financial reporting.','Enter the asset\'s purchase cost.\nEnter its expected salvage (resale) value.\nEnter its useful life in years.\nClick Calculate.','Annual Depreciation = (Cost − Salvage Value) ÷ Useful Life','This method assumes the asset loses value at a constant rate each year — other methods (declining balance, units of production) may better match how some assets actually depreciate.',NULL,'Estimate annual depreciation expense for budgeting or tax purposes.',NULL,'Check with an accountant on which depreciation method your tax jurisdiction requires — straight-line isn\'t always mandatory or optimal.',NULL,''),
(71,'Markup and margin describe the same profit differently — markup is profit as a % of cost, margin is profit as a % of price. This tool shows both from a single cost and price entry.','Enter the product\'s cost.\nEnter the selling price.\nClick Calculate Markup & Margin.','Markup = (Profit ÷ Cost) × 100  •  Margin = (Profit ÷ Price) × 100','The same dollar profit always produces a higher markup % than margin % (except at 0%), since markup divides by the smaller number (cost).',NULL,'Avoid the common markup/margin mix-up when setting prices.',NULL,'When someone says \"50% profit,\" always ask whether they mean markup or margin — they are very different numbers.',NULL,''),
(72,'Accounts receivable aging classifies unpaid invoices by how overdue they are — a standard tool for tracking collections risk.','Enter the invoice date.\nEnter the payment terms in days.\nClick Check Aging Status.','Days Past Due = Today − (Invoice Date + Payment Terms)','Standard aging buckets (current, 1-30, 31-60, 61-90, 90+ days) help prioritize collections follow-up on the oldest, highest-risk invoices first.',NULL,'Prioritize collections calls on the most overdue invoices first.',NULL,'Invoices in the 90+ day bucket carry meaningfully higher default risk — flag them for collections review.',NULL,''),
(73,'Calculate exactly when a bill is due based on its invoice date and standard payment terms like Net 30 or Net 60.','Enter the invoice date.\nSelect the payment terms.\nClick Calculate Due Date.','Due Date = Invoice Date + Payment Terms (days)','Payment terms are a straightforward calendar-day addition from the invoice date, unless your supplier agreement specifies working days instead.',NULL,'Schedule payments on time and avoid late fees or damaged supplier relationships.',NULL,'Set payment reminders a few days before the due date to allow for processing time.',NULL,''),
(74,'Working capital measures a company\'s short-term financial health — the funds available to cover day-to-day operations after paying short-term obligations.','Enter total current assets.\nEnter total current liabilities.\nClick Calculate Working Capital.','Working Capital = Current Assets − Current Liabilities','Positive working capital means a company can cover its short-term obligations; negative working capital can signal a liquidity problem.',NULL,'Quickly gauge a company\'s short-term financial health during a review or audit.',NULL,'Compare working capital trend over time, not just a single snapshot, for a fuller liquidity picture.',NULL,''),
(75,'Return on Equity (ROE) measures how much profit a company generates for every dollar of shareholders\' equity — a key profitability metric for investors.','Enter net income.\nEnter average shareholders\' equity for the period.\nClick Calculate ROE.','ROE = (Net Income ÷ Shareholders\' Equity) × 100','Using the average equity over the period (rather than a single point-in-time figure) gives a more accurate ROE when equity changed significantly during the period.',NULL,'Compare profitability efficiency across companies of different sizes.',NULL,'A high ROE driven by heavy debt (high leverage) carries more risk than one driven by strong operating profitability — check the debt-to-equity ratio alongside ROE.',NULL,''),
(76,'Budget variance analysis compares actual results to planned figures, showing both the dollar difference and percentage over/under budget — a core tool in management accounting and audits.','Enter the budgeted (planned) amount.\nEnter the actual amount.\nClick Calculate Variance.','Variance = Actual − Budget; Variance % = (Variance ÷ |Budget|) × 100','A positive variance in a cost line item usually signals overspending, while a positive variance in a revenue line item signals outperformance — context matters.',NULL,'Flag areas needing management attention during budget reviews.',NULL,'Always interpret variance direction in context — \"over budget\" is bad for costs but good for revenue.',NULL,''),
(77,'URL encoding converts characters that aren\'t safe in URLs (spaces, ampersands, etc.) into a percent-encoded format so they can be safely included in query strings and paths.','Paste text or a URL.\nClick Encode URL or Decode URL.','N/A — this is an encoding/decoding tool, not a calculation.','Reserved and unsafe characters are replaced with a % followed by their hex code, e.g. a space becomes %20.',NULL,'Debug malformed query strings or safely construct URLs with dynamic values.',NULL,'Always encode user-provided values before inserting them into a URL to avoid breaking the URL structure.',NULL,''),
(78,'JSON Web Tokens (JWTs) encode a header and payload as Base64URL — this tool decodes both so you can inspect claims like expiry, issuer, and custom fields without a server round-trip.','Paste a JWT (three dot-separated parts).\nClick Decode JWT.\nInspect the header and payload JSON.','N/A — this is a decoding tool, not a calculation.','A JWT has three parts — header.payload.signature — each Base64URL-encoded; this tool decodes the first two but does NOT verify the signature.',NULL,'Debug authentication issues by inspecting token claims without a backend.',NULL,'This tool only decodes — it never verifies the signature, so a decoded token displaying here is not proof the token is valid or unexpired.',NULL,''),
(79,'Test a regular expression pattern against sample text directly in your browser using JavaScript\'s native regex engine, and see every match instantly.','Enter your regex pattern (without the slashes).\nEnter any flags (e.g. g for global, i for case-insensitive).\nEnter test text.\nClick Test Regex.','N/A — this is a pattern-testing tool, not a calculation.','This uses JavaScript\'s RegExp engine directly, so results match exactly what you\'d get running the same pattern in browser or Node.js JavaScript.',NULL,'Debug and refine regex patterns before using them in production code.',NULL,'Add the \"g\" (global) flag to see every match in the text, not just the first one.',NULL,''),
(80,'Generate a cryptographic hash of any text using the browser\'s native Web Crypto API — useful for verifying data integrity or generating deterministic identifiers.','Enter the text to hash.\nSelect a hash algorithm.\nClick Generate Hash.','N/A — this is a cryptographic hash function, not a calculation.','SHA-256 and its variants are one-way functions: the same input always produces the same hash, but the hash cannot be reversed back to the original input.',NULL,'Verify file/text integrity or generate consistent identifiers from input data.',NULL,'MD5 is not offered here since it\'s cryptographically broken and not supported by the browser\'s secure hashing API — use SHA-256 or higher for anything security-sensitive.',NULL,''),
(81,'HTML entity encoding converts special characters (like <, >, &) into their safe HTML entity equivalents so they display as literal text instead of being interpreted as markup.','Paste text or HTML.\nClick Encode Entities or Decode Entities.','N/A — this is an encoding/decoding tool, not a calculation.','Characters like < and > become &lt; and &gt; so browsers render them as visible text rather than parsing them as HTML tags.',NULL,'Safely display user-generated content or code snippets on a webpage without breaking the layout.',NULL,'Always encode untrusted user input before inserting it into HTML to prevent XSS (cross-site scripting) vulnerabilities.',NULL,''),
(82,'Generate placeholder \"Lorem Ipsum\" text of any length for use in design mockups, wireframes, or testing layouts before real content is available.','Enter how many paragraphs, sentences, or words you need.\nSelect the unit.\nClick Generate Lorem Ipsum.','N/A — this is a text-generation tool, not a calculation.','Text is randomly assembled from the traditional Lorem Ipsum word set, which has been used as neutral filler text in publishing and design since the 1500s.',NULL,'Fill mockups and prototypes with realistic-length text before final copy is ready.',NULL,'Use word count (not just paragraph count) when you need to test a layout with a very specific text length.',NULL,''),
(83,'Reformat a dense, single-line SQL query into a readable, multi-line format with standard keyword capitalization.','Paste your SQL query.\nClick Format SQL.','N/A — this is a text-formatting tool, not a calculation.','Major clauses (FROM, WHERE, JOIN, ORDER BY, etc.) are placed on new lines and keywords are capitalized, matching common SQL style conventions.',NULL,'Make dense, minified SQL queries readable during debugging or code review.',NULL,'This is a lightweight formatter for readability — it does not validate that your SQL syntax is correct.',NULL,''),
(84,'Convert an image directly into a Base64-encoded data URL, letting you embed small images directly in HTML/CSS/JSON without a separate file request.','Choose an image file (under 5MB).\nClick Convert to Base64.\nCopy the resulting data URL.','N/A — this is an encoding tool, not a calculation.','Base64 encoding increases file size by roughly 33% compared to the original binary, so this is best suited to small icons and images, not large photos.',NULL,'Embed small images inline without an extra HTTP request — useful for icons, email templates, or CSS backgrounds.',NULL,'Only use this for genuinely small images (icons, logos) — Base64-encoding large photos bloats your HTML/CSS considerably.',NULL,''),
(85,'Compress JPEG/PNG images directly in your browser using the Canvas API — no upload to a server required — trading a small amount of quality for a smaller file size.','Choose an image file.\nAdjust the quality slider.\nClick Compress Image.\nDownload the compressed result.','N/A — this uses the browser\'s built-in image re-encoding, not a mathematical calculation.','Lowering JPEG quality increases compression by discarding fine image detail the human eye is less sensitive to; PNG compression is lossless so quality has less effect there.',NULL,'Shrink image file sizes for faster website loading without leaving your browser.',NULL,'A quality setting of 60-80% is usually visually indistinguishable from the original for most photos while meaningfully reducing file size.',NULL,''),
(86,'Resize an image to specific pixel dimensions directly in your browser, with an option to lock the aspect ratio so the image doesn\'t distort.','Choose an image file.\nEnter your target width (and height, or lock the ratio).\nClick Resize Image.\nDownload the result.','N/A — this uses Canvas-based image scaling, not a mathematical calculation.','With aspect ratio locked, height is automatically calculated as Width ÷ Original Aspect Ratio to prevent stretching or squashing.',NULL,'Resize images for social media, websites, or email without design software.',NULL,'Keep aspect ratio locked unless you specifically want to distort the image — unlocking it can stretch faces and objects unnaturally.',NULL,''),
(87,'Generate a full tonal palette (light to dark) from a single base color — useful for building consistent UI color scales for buttons, backgrounds, and text.','Enter or pick a base HEX color.\nClick Generate Palette.\nCopy any swatch\'s color value.','Palette shades = same Hue and Saturation as the base color, at varying Lightness levels (90%, 75%, 60%, 45%, 30%, 15%)','Keeping hue and saturation constant while varying only lightness produces a visually cohesive tonal scale, similar to how Tailwind CSS or Material Design color scales are built.',NULL,'Build a consistent color system for a website or app from one brand color.',NULL,'Use the lighter shades for backgrounds and the darker shades for text/borders to maintain accessible contrast.',NULL,''),
(88,'Visually build a CSS linear gradient between two colors at any angle, then copy the ready-to-use CSS code.','Pick your two gradient colors.\nSet the angle.\nClick Generate Gradient.\nCopy the CSS code shown.','CSS: linear-gradient(angle deg, color1 0%, color2 100%)','The angle determines the gradient direction — 0deg is bottom-to-top, 90deg is left-to-right, 180deg is top-to-bottom, matching CSS\'s linear-gradient() angle convention.',NULL,'Design and preview gradients visually instead of guessing CSS values.',NULL,'Gradients between two colors close in hue tend to look smoother than gradients between very different hues.',NULL,''),
(89,'Find out how large an image can be printed at a target resolution (DPI) before it starts to look pixelated.','Enter the image\'s pixel width and height.\nSelect your target print DPI.\nClick Calculate Print Size.','Print Size (inches) = Pixel Dimension ÷ DPI','300 DPI is the standard for close-up print quality; lower DPI is acceptable for large-format prints viewed from further away, like posters and banners.',NULL,'Know the maximum print size before ordering a physical print or poster.',NULL,'For large banners or posters viewed from a distance, 100-150 DPI is often perfectly fine — 300 DPI is unnecessary and would require an enormous image file.',NULL,''),
(90,'Convert measurements between pixels and physical units (inches, centimeters, millimeters), accounting for your chosen DPI/resolution.','Enter the value to convert.\nSelect the units to convert from and to.\nEnter the DPI (only relevant for pixel conversions).\nClick Convert.','Inches = Pixels ÷ DPI; other units convert from inches using standard conversion factors (1in = 2.54cm = 25.4mm)','Pixels are not a fixed physical size — their real-world size depends entirely on the DPI (dots per inch) you specify, unlike inches/cm/mm which are fixed physical units.',NULL,'Convert design measurements accurately between screen and print units.',NULL,'Web design typically uses 96 DPI; print design typically uses 300 DPI — use the right one for your context.',NULL,''),
(91,'Calculate the exact playback duration of a video clip from its total frame count and frame rate — the reverse calculation of finding frame count from duration.','Enter the total frame count.\nSelect the frame rate.\nClick Calculate Duration.','Duration (seconds) = Total Frames ÷ Frame Rate (FPS)','This is the direct inverse of frame count = duration × fps, useful when you know a clip\'s frame count from editing software but need its playback time.',NULL,'Verify clip timing during editing or when specifying exact frame-accurate cuts.',NULL,'Double-check the frame rate your footage was actually shot at — mismatched fps assumptions are a common source of timing errors.',NULL,''),
(92,'A quick reference for YouTube\'s recommended image dimensions across thumbnails, channel banners, icons and Shorts, so you design assets at the correct size the first time.','Select the YouTube asset type you\'re designing.\nClick Get Recommended Size.','N/A — this is a reference lookup, not a calculation.','Dimensions reflect YouTube\'s current published creator guidelines at time of writing; YouTube occasionally updates these specifications.',NULL,'Avoid YouTube cropping or compressing your thumbnails and banners due to incorrect sizing.',NULL,'Channel banners need extra \"safe area\" margin since YouTube crops differently across TV, desktop and mobile — always design within the safe area.',NULL,''),
(93,'Estimate the total storage required for a library of video files based on average file size and count — useful for planning drive or cloud storage purchases.','Enter the average file size per video (GB).\nEnter the number of videos.\nOptionally enter your available storage to check if it fits.\nClick Calculate.','Total Storage = Average Size per Video × Number of Videos','This is a straightforward multiplication; for more precision, calculate average size from a representative sample of your actual footage rather than guessing.',NULL,'Plan storage purchases before a shoot or archive project runs out of space mid-way.',NULL,'Always add 15-20% buffer beyond the calculated minimum for project files, exports, and unexpected re-shoots.',NULL,''),
(94,'Estimate upload time for a video file based on its size and your internet upload speed — handy for planning around long uploads to YouTube, cloud storage, or a client.','Enter the file size in GB.\nEnter your upload speed (Mbps or MB/s).\nClick Calculate Upload Time.','Upload Time (seconds) = File Size (MB) ÷ Upload Speed (MB/s)','Internet speeds are usually advertised in Mbps (megabits), while file sizes are in MB (megabytes) — 1 byte = 8 bits, so Mbps is divided by 8 to get MB/s.',NULL,'Plan your schedule around long uploads instead of guessing.',NULL,'Actual upload speed is often lower than advertised — use your measured real-world speed from a speed test for a more accurate estimate.',NULL,''),
(95,'CGPA (Cumulative Grade Point Average) combines your GPA across every completed semester into one overall average, weighted by each semester\'s credit hours.','Enter each completed semester\'s GPA and credit hours.\nAdd rows for additional semesters.\nClick Calculate CGPA.','CGPA = Σ(Semester GPA × Semester Credits) ÷ Σ(Semester Credits)','This weights each semester by its credit load, so a semester with more credit hours has proportionally more influence on the cumulative figure.',NULL,'Track your overall academic standing across your entire program, not just one term.',NULL,'Recalculate each time you complete a new semester to keep your cumulative figure current.',NULL,''),
(96,'Convert a percentage-based score (common in many countries\' grading systems) into an approximate US-style 4.0-scale GPA equivalent.','Enter your percentage score.\nClick Convert to GPA.','Approximate mapping: 90%+ → 4.0 scale top range, 80-89% → 3.0-3.9, 70-79% → 2.0-2.9, 60-69% → 1.0-1.9, below 60% → below 1.0','This uses a common linear approximation — actual conversion scales vary meaningfully between institutions and countries, so always check with the receiving institution for official conversions.',NULL,'Get a quick estimate when applying to a school that uses a different grading scale than your own.',NULL,'For official applications, request an authoritative conversion from your target institution rather than relying solely on this estimate.',NULL,''),
(97,'Calculate your overall course grade by combining multiple weighted components — assignments, midterms, finals — exactly as most course syllabi define final grades.','Enter each graded component\'s name, score, and weight.\nAdd rows for additional components.\nClick Calculate Final Grade.','Final Grade = Σ(Component Score × Component Weight) ÷ Σ(Weights)','If your entered weights don\'t total exactly 100%, the tool normalizes proportionally so the result is still a valid percentage.',NULL,'Know exactly what you need on remaining assignments to hit a target grade.',NULL,'Enter every graded component from your syllabus, including ones you haven\'t completed yet, to see what score you\'d need on them.',NULL,''),
(98,'Calculate the exact days, hours and minutes remaining until an exam, deadline, graduation, or any other important date.','Pick the target date and time.\nClick Calculate Countdown.','Time Remaining = Target Date/Time − Current Date/Time','The result breaks the remaining time into days, hours and minutes based on your device\'s current local time.',NULL,'Plan study time or project work backward from a firm deadline.',NULL,'For recurring checks, bookmark this page with your date pre-set, or use the live Countdown Timer tool instead for a continuously updating display.',NULL,''),
(99,'A general-purpose scientific calculator supporting trigonometric functions, logarithms, powers and roots, and the constants π and e — for coursework, homework, or everyday calculations.','Click the number and operator buttons to build an expression.\nUse sin/cos/tan/log/ln for scientific functions.\nClick = to evaluate, or C to clear.','Standard order of operations (PEMDAS) applies to all entered expressions.','Trigonometric functions operate in radians, consistent with JavaScript\'s Math library and most scientific calculators\' default mode.',NULL,'Handle scientific and engineering calculations without installing separate calculator software.',NULL,'Remember trig functions here use radians, not degrees — convert degrees to radians (× π/180) first if needed.',NULL,''),
(100,'Calculate the number of cement bags needed for a given concrete volume and mix ratio (cement:sand:aggregate).','Enter the total concrete volume needed.\nSelect your mix ratio.\nClick Calculate Cement Needed.','Cement Volume = (Wet Volume × 1.54) × (Cement Ratio ÷ Total Ratio Parts); Bags = Cement Volume ÷ 1.25 cu ft per bag','The 1.54 factor converts wet (poured) concrete volume to dry material volume, accounting for the volume reduction that occurs when dry materials are mixed with water.',NULL,'Order the right number of cement bags for a pour without over- or under-buying.',NULL,'Always round up to the nearest full bag and add a small contingency for spillage.',NULL,''),
(101,'Calculate the volume (and approximate weight) of sand needed for a given concrete pour and mix ratio.','Enter the total concrete volume needed.\nSelect your mix ratio.\nClick Calculate Sand Needed.','Sand Volume = (Wet Volume × 1.54) × (Sand Ratio ÷ Total Ratio Parts)','Like cement, sand volume is calculated from the dry-material-adjusted (×1.54) total volume, proportioned by its share of the mix ratio.',NULL,'Order the correct quantity of sand alongside cement and aggregate for a concrete pour.',NULL,'Sand bulk density varies with moisture content — the tonnage figure here is an approximation; ask your supplier for their specific material\'s density if precision matters.',NULL,''),
(102,'Calculate how much gravel you need to cover an area at a given depth — commonly used for driveways, paths, and drainage base layers.','Enter the area\'s length and width.\nEnter the desired gravel depth.\nClick Calculate Gravel Needed.','Volume (cu yd) = (Length × Width × Depth in feet) ÷ 27; Tons ≈ Volume × 1.4','The 1.4 tons-per-cubic-yard figure is a typical average for compacted gravel, though it varies by stone size and type — confirm with your supplier for precise ordering.',NULL,'Order the right amount of gravel for a driveway, path, or drainage layer.',NULL,'A 3-4 inch depth is typical for a residential driveway base layer — deeper for heavier vehicle traffic.',NULL,''),
(103,'Calculate the total weight of steel reinforcement bar (rebar) from its standard bar size and total linear length — needed for material ordering and structural load estimates.','Select the rebar size (#3 through #8).\nEnter the total length needed.\nClick Calculate Rebar Weight.','Total Weight = Weight per Foot (standard for bar size) × Total Length','Weight-per-foot values used here are standard ASTM figures for each rebar size (e.g. #4 bar = 0.668 lbs/ft), based on the bar\'s cross-sectional area and steel density.',NULL,'Order the correct tonnage of rebar and estimate structural steel costs.',NULL,'Always confirm exact bar sizing against your structural engineer\'s drawings — using the wrong bar size significantly changes both weight and structural capacity.',NULL,''),
(104,'Calculate the actual paintable/tileable wall area after subtracting the space taken up by doors, windows and other openings.','Enter the wall\'s length and height.\nEnter the total area of doors/windows to subtract.\nClick Calculate Net Wall Area.','Net Area = (Length × Height) − Total Openings Area','Subtracting openings gives the true area needing paint, tile, or wallpaper — using gross area alone typically over-orders material.',NULL,'Order the correct amount of paint, tile, or wallpaper without overbuying.',NULL,'Measure each door/window individually and sum their areas for the most accurate openings total.',NULL,''),
(105,'Calculate the volume of soil to be excavated from a footprint\'s dimensions, and how much more volume that soil occupies once dug up and loosened (\"swell\").','Enter the excavation length, width and depth.\nEnter the expected soil swell factor.\nClick Calculate Excavation Volume.','Bank Volume (cu yd) = (Length × Width × Depth) ÷ 27; Loose Volume = Bank Volume × (1 + Swell %)','\"Bank\" volume is the undisturbed in-ground volume; \"loose\" volume is what that same soil occupies once excavated and loosened, which is always larger due to increased air gaps between particles.',NULL,'Estimate truck loads needed to haul away excavated soil, or fill needed to backfill later.',NULL,'Swell factor varies by soil type — clay swells more (30-40%) than sand or gravel (15-20%); use a figure appropriate to your site\'s soil.',NULL,''),
(106,'Calculate the total cubic volume of one or more wood pieces from their length, width and thickness — useful for material estimating and shipping weight calculations.','Enter the piece\'s length, width and thickness in inches.\nEnter the quantity of identical pieces.\nClick Calculate Wood Volume.','Volume (cu ft) = (Length × Width × Thickness in inches ÷ 1728) × Quantity','Dividing by 1728 (12³) converts cubic inches to cubic feet, the standard unit for bulk lumber volume.',NULL,'Estimate material volume for shipping, storage, or kiln-drying calculations.',NULL,'For pricing by board foot instead of cubic feet, use the dedicated Board Feet Calculator.',NULL,''),
(107,'Calculate the gross amount of material to purchase, accounting for an expected waste percentage from cuts, defects, and offcuts.','Enter the net material actually needed (board feet or sq ft).\nEnter your expected waste percentage.\nClick Calculate Gross Material Needed.','Gross Material = Net Material ÷ (1 − Waste %)','Dividing by (1 − waste%) rather than simply adding waste% on top correctly accounts for waste as a percentage of what you buy, not just what you use.',NULL,'Avoid running short mid-project by buying enough material the first time.',NULL,'Complex cut lists with many angled or small pieces typically need a higher waste allowance (15-20%) than simple straight cuts (5-10%).',NULL,''),
(108,'Calculate the total cost of lumber for a project, including a waste allowance so the budget reflects what you\'ll actually need to purchase.','Enter the board feet needed.\nEnter the price per board foot.\nEnter a waste allowance percentage.\nClick Calculate Total Wood Cost.','Total Cost = (Board Feet × (1 + Waste %)) × Price per Board Foot','Including waste allowance in the cost estimate avoids a mid-project budget surprise when you need to buy more material than the bare minimum calculation suggested.',NULL,'Budget a woodworking project accurately, including realistic material overage.',NULL,'Get current pricing from your specific lumber supplier — prices vary significantly by species, grade, and region.',NULL,''),
(109,'Calculate your vehicle\'s real-world fuel efficiency (miles per gallon) from actual odometer readings and fuel used — more accurate than manufacturer estimates for your specific driving.','Enter your starting odometer reading.\nEnter your ending odometer reading.\nEnter the gallons of fuel used over that distance.\nClick Calculate Mileage.','MPG = (Ending Odometer − Starting Odometer) ÷ Gallons Used','Tracking mileage this way over several fill-ups gives a more accurate real-world MPG figure than the manufacturer\'s EPA estimate, which reflects standardized test conditions.',NULL,'Track your vehicle\'s real fuel efficiency and spot maintenance issues that reduce it over time.',NULL,'Fill the tank completely each time you measure for the most accurate fuel-used figure.',NULL,''),
(110,'Estimate the total cost of a road trip or delivery run, combining fuel cost with tolls, food, and other incidental expenses.','Enter the trip distance.\nEnter your vehicle\'s fuel efficiency (MPG).\nEnter the current fuel price.\nEnter any additional costs (tolls, food).\nClick Calculate.','Total Cost = (Distance ÷ MPG × Fuel Price) + Additional Costs','This extends a simple fuel cost calculation to include the other real expenses a trip typically incurs beyond just fuel.',NULL,'Budget a road trip or estimate delivery run costs more completely than fuel alone.',NULL,'Include a rough estimate for tolls even if you\'re unsure of the exact route — most mapping apps show toll estimates.',NULL,''),
(111,'Calculate the great-circle (straight-line, \"as the crow flies\") distance between two points given their latitude and longitude, using the standard Haversine formula.','Enter Point A\'s latitude and longitude.\nEnter Point B\'s latitude and longitude.\nClick Calculate Distance.','Haversine formula: a = sin²(Δlat/2) + cos(lat1)·cos(lat2)·sin²(Δlon/2); distance = 2R·atan2(√a, √(1−a))','The Haversine formula accounts for the Earth\'s curvature, giving an accurate great-circle distance rather than a flat-plane approximation — but it is straight-line distance, not actual driving/walking route distance.',NULL,'Quickly estimate distance between two locations without a mapping service.',NULL,'This is straight-line distance only — actual road travel distance will be longer, sometimes significantly so.',NULL,''),
(112,'Estimate a delivery\'s arrival time by combining driving time with time spent at intermediate stops — more realistic than distance-and-speed alone for multi-stop routes.','Enter the total route distance.\nEnter the average driving speed.\nEnter the number of stops and time per stop.\nClick Calculate ETA.','Total Time = (Distance ÷ Speed) + (Stops × Time per Stop)','Adding stop time separately from driving time gives a more realistic ETA for multi-stop delivery routes than pure distance/speed alone.',NULL,'Give customers more accurate delivery windows on multi-stop routes.',NULL,'Use a conservative average speed that accounts for traffic and stop-and-go city driving, not highway cruising speed.',NULL,''),
(113,'Convert a specific date and time from one time zone to another — useful for scheduling meetings or calls across different regions.','Pick the date and time.\nSelect the source time zone.\nSelect the target time zone.\nClick Convert Time Zone.','Converted Time = Source Time adjusted by the UTC offset difference between the two zones at that date','This correctly accounts for each zone\'s current daylight saving status at the specific date entered, not just a fixed UTC offset, since offsets can change across the year.',NULL,'Avoid scheduling errors when coordinating meetings across time zones.',NULL,'Daylight saving transitions happen on different dates in different countries — always double-check times scheduled near those transition dates.',NULL,''),
(114,'A live countdown timer that continuously updates in real time down to any future date and time you choose — for events, deadlines, or launches.','Pick the target date and time.\nClick Start Countdown.\nWatch the live display update every second.','Time Remaining = Target Date/Time − Current Time (updated every second)','Unlike a one-time calculation, this timer re-checks the current time every second using your browser\'s clock to keep the display live.',NULL,'Create a live countdown display for an event page or personal deadline tracking.',NULL,'Keep the browser tab open — the countdown stops updating if you navigate away or close the tab.',NULL,''),
(115,'Generate one or more random numbers within any range you specify, with an option to exclude duplicates — useful for raffles, sampling, games, or picking a winner.','Enter the minimum and maximum values.\nEnter how many numbers you need.\nOptionally check \'No duplicates\'.\nClick Generate.','Random Integer = floor(Math.random() × (Max − Min + 1)) + Min','Each number is drawn uniformly at random from the inclusive range you specify; with \"no duplicates\" checked, already-drawn numbers are excluded from subsequent picks.',NULL,'Pick raffle winners, generate test data, or make unbiased random selections.',NULL,'Check \"no duplicates\" when picking multiple unique winners or samples from a fixed pool.',NULL,''),
(116,'ABC analysis ranks inventory items by their annual usage value and splits them into three classes: A (the vital few, highest value), B (moderate value), and C (the many low-value items) — based on the Pareto principle that roughly 80% of value usually comes from about 20% of items.','Enter each item\'s name and its annual usage value (annual quantity × unit cost works well).\nAdd or remove rows as needed.\nClick Run ABC Analysis.','Class A = cumulative % of value ≤ 80%, Class B = next up to 95%, Class C = the remaining items','Items are ranked by value from highest to lowest, then cumulative percentage of total value is tracked down the list to assign each item a class.',NULL,'Focus tighter inventory control, forecasting effort and cycle counts on your highest-value \"A\" items instead of treating every SKU equally.',NULL,'Re-run this periodically — an item\'s class can shift as sales patterns change.',NULL,''),
(117,'Dead stock is inventory that hasn\'t sold or been used in a defined period and is at risk of never moving. This tool flags an item as dead stock once it crosses your chosen \"no sale\" threshold, and shows the capital tied up in it.','Enter the current stock quantity and unit cost.\nEnter how many days it\'s been since the last sale.\nSet your dead stock threshold (90 days is a common starting point).\nClick Check Dead Stock.','Capital Tied Up = Stock Quantity × Unit Cost; Dead Stock if Days Since Last Sale ≥ Threshold','The threshold varies by industry — fast-moving consumer goods might use 30-60 days, while durable goods might use 180+ days.',NULL,'Spot capital trapped in slow-moving inventory early, before it needs to be written off or heavily discounted.',NULL,'Consider a clearance sale or bundling dead stock before it becomes a total write-off.',NULL,''),
(118,'When launching a new or trial product, ordering too little means stockouts and lost momentum; ordering too much risks dead stock on unproven demand. This tool sizes a trial order from your expected demand plus a safety buffer.','Enter your expected weekly demand for the new product.\nEnter the trial period length in weeks.\nSet a safety buffer percentage to cushion for demand uncertainty.\nClick Calculate Trial Order Quantity.','Trial Order Quantity = Expected Weekly Demand × Trial Weeks × (1 + Safety Buffer %)','The safety buffer compensates for the fact that trial demand is a forecast, not a proven track record — a higher buffer suits less-certain estimates.',NULL,'Avoid guessing a trial order size from gut feel alone.',NULL,'Use a smaller buffer (10-15%) for products similar to ones you already sell, and a larger one (25%+) for something genuinely new.',NULL,''),
(119,'Stock holding — also called stock coverage or days of supply — tells you how many days your current stock will last at its current usage rate, and when you can expect to run out.','Enter your current stock quantity.\nEnter your average daily usage.\nClick Calculate Stock Holding.','Days of Coverage = Current Stock ÷ Average Daily Usage','This is a snapshot based on average usage — actual run-out can vary if demand spikes or slows.',NULL,'Know at a glance whether you\'re comfortably stocked or approaching a stockout.',NULL,'Compare days of coverage against your supplier\'s lead time — if coverage is close to lead time, it\'s time to reorder.',NULL,''),
(120,'A quick readiness check before committing to an order: is there enough available stock to fulfill it, once your safety stock reserve is kept aside?','Enter your currently available stock.\nEnter the safety stock you want kept in reserve (0 if none).\nEnter the order quantity you need to fulfill.\nClick Check Order Readiness.','Usable Stock = Available Stock − Safety Stock; Good to Go if Usable Stock ≥ Order Quantity','Keeping safety stock out of the usable pool ensures an order doesn\'t eat into the buffer meant for demand variability or lead-time delays.',NULL,'Avoid promising an order you can\'t actually fulfill without raiding your safety buffer.',NULL,'Set safety stock to 0 if you want to check against total available stock with no reserve held back.',NULL,''),
(121,'A wa.me link opens a WhatsApp chat with a specific number directly — no need for the visitor to save your number first. Add it to your bio, website, business card or ads.','Enter the phone number, with country code.\nOptionally add a pre-filled message.\nClick Generate — copy the link or share it anywhere.','https://wa.me/<countrycode+number>?text=<url-encoded message>','Pakistani numbers starting with 0 are automatically converted to the +92 country code format WhatsApp requires.',NULL,'One link works everywhere — social bio, email signature, printed materials, ads — with no app or API integration needed.',NULL,'Keep the pre-filled message short and specific, e.g. \"Hi, I saw your ad for [product]\" — it saves the customer typing and gives you context instantly.',NULL,''),
(122,'Skip staring at a blank message box — pick a message type, fill in a few details, and get a ready-to-send WhatsApp message every time.','Choose the type of message you need.\nEnter the customer name, your business name, and any relevant detail (amount, order number, etc).\nOptionally add the customer\'s phone to also get a direct WhatsApp link.\nClick Generate Message.','Template-based generation — each message type fills a proven message structure with your details.','',NULL,'Consistent, professional messaging across your whole team, without everyone writing from scratch each time.',NULL,'Save your favorite generated messages somewhere handy (notes app) so you can reuse and tweak them for similar situations.',NULL,''),
(123,'A clear, polite payment reminder gets paid faster than an awkward phone call. Fill in the details and get a ready-to-send message.','Enter the customer name and amount due.\nChoose the payment method you\'d like them to use.\nOptionally add account details and a due date.\nClick Generate.','','',NULL,'Consistent, professional reminders reduce awkwardness and speed up collections.',NULL,'Sending reminders a few days before the due date (not just after) often gets faster payment than a reminder sent only once it\'s overdue.',NULL,''),
(124,'Quickly work out how much commission a salesperson earns on a given sales amount, with an optional fixed base pay added on top.','Enter the total sales amount.\nEnter the commission rate (%).\nOptionally enter a fixed base pay.\nClick Calculate Commission.','Commission = Sales Amount × (Rate ÷ 100); Total Pay = Commission + Base Pay','',NULL,'Transparent, consistent commission calculations for payroll and salesperson motivation.',NULL,'Tiered commission structures (higher rate above a threshold) need to be calculated per tier — this tool handles a single flat rate.',NULL,''),
(125,'See exactly how much you need to sell per remaining working day to hit your monthly target, based on what you\'ve already achieved.','Enter your monthly sales target.\nEnter what you\'ve achieved so far this month.\nEnter how many working days remain.\nClick Calculate.','Required Daily Sales = (Target − Achieved) ÷ Working Days Remaining','',NULL,'Turns a big monthly number into a clear, actionable daily number.',NULL,'Recalculate every few days — your required daily pace changes as you sell (or as days pass).',NULL,''),
(126,'Customer Acquisition Cost (CAC) tells you how much you\'re spending, on average, to win each new customer — essential for judging whether your marketing spend is paying off.','Enter your total sales & marketing spend for the period.\nEnter how many new customers you acquired.\nClick Calculate CAC.','CAC = Total Sales & Marketing Spend ÷ New Customers Acquired','',NULL,'Compare CAC against Customer Lifetime Value (CLV) — if CAC is close to or higher than CLV, your acquisition spend isn\'t sustainable.',NULL,'Track CAC by channel (Facebook ads vs referrals vs walk-in) separately to see which is actually most cost-effective.',NULL,''),
(127,'Customer Lifetime Value (CLV) estimates the total revenue a typical customer brings in over the whole time they stay with you — key for deciding how much you can afford to spend acquiring them.','Enter the average order value.\nEnter how many times a customer purchases per year.\nEnter how many years a customer typically stays.\nClick Calculate.','CLV = Average Order Value × Purchases per Year × Customer Lifespan (years)','This is the standard simplified CLV formula — more advanced versions also factor in profit margin and a discount rate for future revenue.',NULL,'Sets a ceiling for sensible customer acquisition spend — compare it against your CAC.',NULL,'Segment CLV by customer type if you have very different customer groups (e.g. wholesale vs retail) — a blended average can be misleading.',NULL,''),
(128,'Average Order Value (AOV) shows how much customers typically spend per order — a key metric for judging upselling, bundling and pricing strategies.','Enter total revenue for the period.\nEnter the number of orders.\nClick Calculate AOV.','AOV = Total Revenue ÷ Number of Orders','',NULL,'Track AOV over time to see if upselling, bundling or minimum-order incentives are working.',NULL,'A rising AOV with a stable order count usually means customers are buying more per visit — a strong sign your upsell/bundle strategy is working.',NULL,''),
(129,'Conversion rate shows what share of your leads, visitors, or inquiries actually become paying customers — a core metric for judging your sales process.','Enter your total leads or visitors.\nEnter how many converted into customers.\nClick Calculate.','Conversion Rate = (Converted ÷ Total Leads) × 100','',NULL,'Pinpoint whether a low-sales period is a lead-volume problem or a lead-quality/sales-process problem.',NULL,'Track conversion rate separately by lead source — some channels convert far better than others even with fewer total leads.',NULL,''),
(130,'Return on Ad Spend (ROAS) shows how much revenue you generate for every rupee spent on advertising — a key metric for judging whether an ad campaign is worth running.','Enter the revenue generated from the ad campaign.\nEnter the total ad spend.\nClick Calculate ROAS.','ROAS = Revenue ÷ Ad Spend','',NULL,'Compare campaigns on a like-for-like basis, regardless of how much each one spent.',NULL,'ROAS looks at revenue, not profit — a high ROAS on a low-margin product can still be unprofitable, so check it alongside your margins.',NULL,''),
(131,'Compare sales between two periods (month-over-month, year-over-year) to see your growth rate as a clean percentage.','Enter the previous period\'s sales.\nEnter the current period\'s sales.\nClick Calculate Growth.','Growth % = ((Current − Previous) ÷ Previous) × 100','',NULL,'A single, comparable number for tracking trend direction over time or across teams/regions.',NULL,'Compare like periods (e.g. this January vs last January) rather than adjacent months, to avoid mistaking seasonal swings for real growth or decline.',NULL,''),
(132,'Work out the monthly installment amount for a customer paying in parts, after a down payment, with an optional markup for offering the installment plan.','Enter the total price.\nEnter the down payment (if any).\nEnter the number of monthly installments.\nOptionally add a markup % for the installment plan.\nClick Calculate.','Monthly Installment = ((Price − Down Payment) × (1 + Markup%)) ÷ Number of Installments','',NULL,'Quote clear, accurate installment terms to customers on the spot.',NULL,'A small markup (5-15%) is common to offset the risk and delayed cash flow of offering installment plans.',NULL,''),
(133,'Set consistent wholesale and retail prices from a single product cost, using different margin percentages for each channel.','Enter the product cost.\nEnter your target wholesale margin %.\nEnter your target retail margin %.\nClick Calculate Prices.','Price = Cost × (1 + Margin% ÷ 100)','',NULL,'Keep pricing consistent across channels without recalculating from scratch for every product.',NULL,'Retail margins are typically higher than wholesale margins since retail involves more marketing, smaller order sizes and higher per-unit selling effort.',NULL,''),
(134,'Price a product bundle by applying a discount to the combined price of the individual items — and see exactly how much the customer saves.','Add up what the items would cost bought separately.\nEnter your bundle discount percentage.\nClick Calculate Bundle Price.','Bundle Price = Sum of Item Prices × (1 − Discount% ÷ 100)','',NULL,'Clearly communicate bundle savings to customers, which is often a stronger sales driver than the bundle price alone.',NULL,'Highlighting the rupee amount saved (not just the % discount) tends to be more persuasive in your marketing copy.',NULL,''),
(135,'Calculate sales tax, GST or VAT on a sale amount — whether that amount already includes tax or needs tax added on top.','Enter the sale amount.\nEnter your applicable tax rate.\nChoose whether the amount already includes tax or not.\nClick Calculate Tax.','Tax-exclusive: Tax = Amount × Rate; Tax-inclusive: Net = Amount ÷ (1 + Rate), Tax = Amount − Net','Tax-inclusive calculations back out the tax already baked into a total price — a different formula than simply adding tax on top.',NULL,'Avoid the common mistake of adding tax on top of an already tax-inclusive price.',NULL,'Always confirm your exact applicable rate with your tax authority (e.g. FBR for Pakistan) — rates vary by product category, province and business type.',NULL,''),
(136,'Cash-on-Delivery profit looks smaller once delivery charges and refused/returned orders are factored in — this tool shows your real per-order profit.','Enter the product\'s sale price and cost.\nEnter the courier/delivery charge.\nOptionally enter your typical return/refusal rate.\nClick Calculate.','Profit per Delivered Order = Sale Price − Cost − Delivery Charge','When a return rate is set, expected profit per order placed also subtracts the cost+delivery lost on returned/refused orders.',NULL,'See your true COD profitability, not just the on-paper margin before delivery and returns.',NULL,'Track your actual return/refusal rate over a few weeks of orders — it\'s often higher than expected and significantly affects real profitability.',NULL,''),
(137,'Estimate delivery cost for a package using a typical courier pricing structure — a base rate for the first kg, plus a per-kg rate for the rest, with an optional COD handling fee.','Enter the package weight.\nEnter the base rate (first kg) and rate per additional kg.\nOptionally add a COD handling fee % and order value.\nClick Calculate.','Cost = Base Rate + (Weight − 1kg) × Extra Rate per kg, plus COD fee if applicable','',NULL,'Quickly estimate shipping cost for pricing decisions or comparing courier rate cards.',NULL,'Courier rate cards vary a lot — plug in your actual courier\'s base rate and per-kg rate for an accurate estimate specific to your provider.',NULL,''),
(138,'A structured cold call script helps new and experienced salespeople alike open calls confidently and consistently.','Enter your name, company, product/service and main customer benefit.\nClick Generate Cold Call Script.\nCopy and personalize it before your call.','','',NULL,'A consistent opening reduces awkward pauses and helps your whole team sound professional from the first sentence.',NULL,'Practice the script out loud a few times — it should sound natural, not read word-for-word off the screen.',NULL,''),
(139,'Generate a concise, structured pitch built around your target customer\'s problem and your product\'s key benefit — useful for calls, meetings, or written proposals.','Enter your product/service, target customer, the problem it solves, and its key benefit.\nClick Generate Sales Pitch.\nCopy and adapt it to your own voice.','','',NULL,'A ready starting point that follows a proven problem-benefit-close structure.',NULL,'Keep pitches under 30 seconds when spoken out loud — if it takes longer, you\'re probably including too much detail for an opening pitch.',NULL,''),
(140,'Common objections (\"too expensive\", \"need to think about it\", \"using a competitor\") come up constantly — this generates a proven response structure for each, personalized with your product\'s advantage.','Select the objection you\'re hearing.\nEnter your product\'s key advantage.\nClick Generate Response.\nAdapt the wording to sound like you.','','',NULL,'Never get caught off guard by a common objection again — have a structured response ready.',NULL,'The best objection handling acknowledges the concern first, then reframes — resist the urge to argue or dismiss the objection outright.',NULL,'');
INSERT INTO `tool_faqs` (`id`, `tool_id`, `question`, `answer`, `sort_order`, `status`) VALUES (1,1,'Does this account for progressive tax brackets?','Not directly — enter your effective (blended) tax rate for an accurate result, since this tool applies a single flat rate.',0,'published'),
(2,1,'Can I use this for hourly wages?','Yes — multiply your hourly rate by hours worked in the period to get a gross salary figure first.',1,'published'),
(3,2,'What multiplier should I use?','Time-and-a-half (1.5x) is standard for overtime in the US; double time (2x) often applies to holidays or hours beyond a higher threshold — check your employer\'s policy or local law.',0,'published'),
(4,2,'Does this include my regular pay too?','No, this calculates only the overtime premium pay; add it to your regular pay for total gross pay.',1,'published'),
(5,3,'Does this handle overnight shifts?','Yes — if your end time is earlier than your start time, the tool automatically treats it as an overnight shift.',0,'published'),
(6,3,'Should I include my lunch break?','Only if it is unpaid — enter unpaid break minutes to have them subtracted from the total.',1,'published'),
(7,4,'Does this exclude public holidays?','No, only weekends (Saturday and Sunday) are excluded — public holidays vary too much by country to build in reliably.',0,'published'),
(8,4,'Is the start date included in the count?','Yes, both the start and end dates are included if they fall on a weekday.',1,'published'),
(9,5,'What if my demand fluctuates seasonally?','EOQ assumes constant demand; for highly seasonal products, recalculate EOQ per season or combine it with a safety stock buffer.',0,'published'),
(10,5,'What counts as \"ordering cost\"?','Any fixed cost incurred per order placed — e.g. purchasing admin time, shipping setup fees — not the cost of the goods themselves.',1,'published'),
(11,6,'What happens if I set safety stock to zero?','Your reorder point exactly matches expected usage during lead time — any unexpected demand spike or shipping delay could cause a stockout.',0,'published'),
(12,6,'How often should I recalculate this?','Whenever your average usage rate or supplier lead time changes meaningfully, e.g. seasonally or after switching suppliers.',1,'published'),
(13,7,'What if my max and average scenarios are the same?','Then your calculated safety stock will be zero — demand and lead time are stable enough that no extra buffer is mathematically indicated by this model.',0,'published'),
(14,7,'Does this replace demand forecasting?','No — it complements it. Better forecasts narrow the gap between average and maximum usage, which lowers the required safety stock.',1,'published'),
(15,8,'What is a \"good\" turnover ratio?','It varies widely by industry — grocery retailers often see 10x+ per year, while heavy equipment sellers may see 2-4x. Compare against your own industry benchmark.',0,'published'),
(16,8,'How do I calculate average inventory?','Add your beginning and ending inventory value for the period and divide by two.',1,'published'),
(17,9,'Does this include fees or insurance?','No — this calculates principal and interest only. Add any origination fees, insurance or taxes separately for your full payment.',0,'published'),
(18,9,'What if the interest rate is 0%?','The tool correctly falls back to a simple division of principal by the number of months.',1,'published'),
(19,10,'What compounding frequency should I choose?','Use whatever your bank or investment account actually applies — check your account terms; monthly is common for savings accounts.',0,'published'),
(20,10,'Does this account for additional contributions?','No, this calculates growth of a single lump sum only — it does not model recurring deposits.',1,'published'),
(21,11,'When is simple interest used?','Common in short-term loans, certain bonds, and some auto loans — check your loan agreement to see which method applies.',0,'published'),
(22,11,'Can I use months instead of years?','Yes — convert months to years by dividing by 12 before entering the time period (e.g. 6 months = 0.5).',1,'published'),
(23,12,'What\'s the difference between margin and markup?','Margin is profit divided by revenue (selling price); markup is profit divided by cost. The same transaction produces two different percentages depending on which you use.',0,'published'),
(24,12,'What is a healthy profit margin?','It varies hugely by industry — retail often runs 20-50%, while software/SaaS businesses can exceed 70-80%.',1,'published'),
(25,13,'What counts as a fixed cost?','Costs that don\'t change with production volume — rent, salaries, insurance — as opposed to variable costs like materials, which scale per unit.',0,'published'),
(26,13,'What if I have multiple products?','Calculate a blended average selling price and variable cost across your product mix, or run this calculator separately per product line.',1,'published'),
(27,14,'What is a good current ratio?','Generally 1.5–3 is considered healthy, but the ideal figure depends heavily on the industry and business model.',0,'published'),
(28,14,'What does a ratio below 1 mean?','It suggests the company may struggle to cover short-term liabilities with short-term assets, which can be a liquidity red flag.',1,'published'),
(29,15,'Why exclude inventory?','Inventory can take time to sell and may not convert to cash at its full book value, so excluding it gives a more conservative liquidity picture.',0,'published'),
(30,15,'How is this different from the current ratio?','The current ratio includes all current assets (including inventory); the quick ratio strips inventory out for a stricter test.',1,'published'),
(31,16,'What is considered a high D/E ratio?','It varies by industry; a ratio above 2 is often flagged as high leverage in most sectors, but capital-intensive industries commonly run higher.',0,'published'),
(32,16,'Does a low D/E ratio mean a company is healthier?','Not necessarily — very low leverage can also mean a company isn\'t using debt efficiently to fund growth.',1,'published'),
(33,17,'Why is EBITDA used instead of net income?','It strips out financing and accounting decisions (debt levels, tax jurisdiction, depreciation method) to better compare core operating performance across companies.',0,'published'),
(34,17,'Is EBITDA the same as cash flow?','No — it\'s an approximation. It ignores changes in working capital and capital expenditures, both of which affect real cash flow.',1,'published'),
(35,18,'Is my JSON data sent to a server?','No — all formatting and validation happens entirely in your browser using JavaScript; nothing is uploaded.',0,'published'),
(36,18,'Why does it say my JSON is invalid?','Common causes are trailing commas, single quotes instead of double quotes, or unquoted keys — valid JSON requires double-quoted keys and strings.',1,'published'),
(37,19,'Is Base64 encryption?','No — it is a reversible encoding scheme, not encryption. Anyone can decode it back to the original text.',0,'published'),
(38,19,'Why did decoding fail?','The input likely isn\'t valid Base64 — check for missing characters, extra whitespace, or that it was actually Base64-encoded.',1,'published'),
(39,20,'Is this password sent anywhere?','No — generation happens entirely in your browser and nothing is transmitted or stored.',0,'published'),
(40,20,'How long should my password be?','Most security guidance now recommends at least 12-16 characters, longer for highly sensitive accounts.',1,'published'),
(41,21,'Are these UUIDs guaranteed unique?','Practically yes — the probability of collision is astronomically small (about 1 in 2^122) given the random bit space used.',0,'published'),
(42,21,'What UUID version does this generate?','Version 4, the random-based variant, which is the most commonly used UUID type for general-purpose unique IDs.',1,'published'),
(43,22,'Is this seconds or milliseconds?','This tool expects seconds (the standard Unix timestamp) — if your source uses milliseconds, divide by 1000 first.',0,'published'),
(44,22,'What timezone is the converted date shown in?','UTC, to avoid ambiguity — convert to your local timezone using your own system clock or a timezone converter if needed.',1,'published'),
(45,23,'What if I enter both a new width and height?','The tool prioritizes the new width field — clear it if you want to scale from height instead.',0,'published'),
(46,23,'What is a common video aspect ratio?','16:9 is standard for widescreen video; 9:16 is standard for vertical mobile/story content.',1,'published'),
(47,24,'Is the CMYK conversion print-accurate?','It\'s a standard mathematical approximation from RGB — for professional print jobs, verify against your printer\'s specific ICC color profile.',0,'published'),
(48,24,'What is HSL useful for?','HSL (Hue, Saturation, Lightness) makes it easy to create color variations — e.g. lighter/darker shades — by adjusting just the lightness value.',1,'published'),
(49,25,'What DPI do I need for professional printing?','300 DPI is the standard target for close-up print materials like brochures and photos.',0,'published'),
(50,25,'Is DPI the same as PPI?','They\'re often used interchangeably in casual use, though technically PPI (pixels per inch) refers to screen/image density and DPI to physical printer dot density.',1,'published'),
(51,26,'Do these sizes change over time?','Yes — platforms periodically update their specifications, so double-check the platform\'s official documentation for business-critical assets.',0,'published'),
(52,26,'What if my image is a different aspect ratio?','The platform will typically crop or letterbox it — design at the exact recommended size to keep full control over composition.',1,'published'),
(53,27,'Why might my actual file be a different size?','Codecs using variable bitrate (VBR) encoding fluctuate the actual bitrate throughout the video, so real file size can differ from a constant-bitrate estimate.',0,'published'),
(54,27,'Should I include audio bitrate?','Yes, for an accurate total — video-only bitrate alone will underestimate final file size.',1,'published'),
(55,28,'Why is my exported file slightly bigger than my target?','Encoders using variable bitrate can briefly exceed the average target during complex scenes — leave a small margin below hard limits.',0,'published'),
(56,28,'What if the result is negative or very low?','Your target file size is too small for the given duration and audio bitrate — increase the size limit or shorten the video.',1,'published'),
(57,29,'Why does frame rate matter for editing?','Mismatched frame rates between clips can cause stuttering or require conversion — knowing exact frame counts helps align cuts precisely.',0,'published'),
(58,29,'What frame rate should I use for YouTube?','30fps is a common default for standard content; 60fps is preferred for fast motion like gaming or sports.',1,'published'),
(59,30,'Is this an accurate prediction of my actual earnings?','No — it\'s a rough estimate. Actual RPM depends on niche, audience location, ad demand, seasonality, and YouTube\'s revenue share, all of which fluctuate.',0,'published'),
(60,30,'Where do I find my real RPM?','In YouTube Studio → Analytics → Revenue tab, once your channel is monetized and has earnings history.',1,'published'),
(61,31,'Does this use a 4.0 or different scale?','This tool uses the standard US 4.0 scale with +/- grade distinctions; check with your institution if they use a different scale.',0,'published'),
(62,31,'How do I calculate cumulative GPA across semesters?','Include every course from every semester in the same calculation, weighted by each course\'s credit hours.',1,'published'),
(63,32,'Does this match my school\'s exact grading scale?','It uses a common approximation (90/80/70/60% cutoffs) — check your syllabus for your institution\'s precise scale.',0,'published'),
(64,32,'Can I use this for weighted assignments?','Only for a single assignment\'s raw percentage — for a weighted course grade across multiple assignments, sum weighted contributions separately.',1,'published'),
(65,33,'How is \"classes I can still miss\" calculated?','It estimates how many additional classes (beyond the total entered) you could miss while still meeting your target percentage, based on your current attendance count staying fixed.',0,'published'),
(66,33,'What if I\'m already below the required percentage?','The tool instead estimates how many consecutive classes you\'d need to attend without missing any to climb back to the target.',1,'published'),
(67,34,'Can I change the focus and break durations?','Yes — enter your preferred minutes for each before starting; the classic default is 25 minutes focus and 5 minutes break.',0,'published'),
(68,34,'Does the timer keep running if I close the tab?','No — this is a simple browser-based timer with no background/notification support; keep the tab open while using it.',1,'published'),
(69,35,'Should I order extra concrete beyond this calculation?','Yes — most contractors add 5-10% extra to account for spillage, subgrade irregularities, and formwork variance.',0,'published'),
(70,35,'How many 80lb bags equal a cubic yard?','Approximately 45 bags of pre-mixed 80lb concrete make one cubic yard, though this varies slightly by product.',1,'published'),
(71,36,'Does this account for windows and doors?','No — subtract the area of windows and doors from your total wall area before entering it for a more accurate estimate.',0,'published'),
(72,36,'How many coats do I actually need?','Two coats is standard for most projects; dramatic color changes (e.g. dark to light) may need a third coat or a tinted primer.',1,'published'),
(73,37,'What waste percentage should I use?','10% is standard for a straight layout; 15-20% is safer for diagonal layouts or complex room shapes with many cuts.',0,'published'),
(74,37,'Should I buy tiles from the same batch/lot?','Yes — tile color can vary slightly between production batches, so buy your full estimated quantity from the same lot number.',1,'published'),
(75,38,'What if my bricks aren\'t standard modular size?','Adjust the \"bricks per sq ft\" field to match your specific brick dimensions and mortar joint — your supplier can usually provide this figure.',0,'published'),
(76,38,'How much waste allowance is typical?','5-10% is standard for straightforward walls; increase it for complex corners, arches, or patterned brickwork.',1,'published'),
(77,39,'What is a roofing \"square\"?','A unit equal to 100 square feet of roof area, commonly used when ordering shingles and other roofing materials.',0,'published'),
(78,39,'How do I know my roof pitch?','Roof pitch is usually specified as rise over a 12-inch run (e.g. \"6/12\") — check your building plans or measure directly if unsure.',1,'published'),
(79,40,'Is a \"2x4\" actually 2 inches by 4 inches?','No — that\'s the nominal (pre-planing) size. The actual dressed size is typically 1.5\" × 3.5\"; use actual dimensions for accurate board footage.',0,'published'),
(80,40,'Do lumber yards always price by board foot?','Hardwoods are almost always priced by board foot; dimensional softwood lumber (like standard 2x4 studs) is often priced per linear foot instead.',1,'published'),
(81,41,'What is the standard plywood sheet size?','4×8 feet (32 sq ft) is the standard sheet size in North America; adjust the sheet size field if yours differs.',0,'published'),
(82,41,'How much waste allowance should I use?','10% is reasonable for simple rectangular cuts; increase to 15-20% for irregular shapes or many small pieces.',1,'published'),
(83,42,'What angle do I use for a standard rectangular frame?','45° — a 4-sided frame divides its 90° corner evenly between two 45° cuts.',0,'published'),
(84,42,'Does this work for irregular (non-regular) polygons?','No — this calculator assumes a regular polygon with equal sides and angles; irregular shapes need each corner calculated individually.',1,'published'),
(85,43,'Why is my actual riser height different from my target?','Because the number of steps must be a whole number, the total rise is divided evenly across that count — the result is very close to, but rarely exactly, your target.',0,'published'),
(86,43,'What riser height is code-compliant?','Most US residential codes require risers between 4 and 7.75 inches, with treads at least 10 inches deep — always verify your local building code.',1,'published'),
(87,44,'Should I use city or highway MPG?','Use whichever best matches your actual route, or a blended average if your trip includes both city and highway driving.',0,'published'),
(88,44,'Does this include tolls or other trip costs?','No — this calculates fuel cost only; add tolls, parking or other expenses separately for total trip cost.',1,'published'),
(89,45,'Why does CBM matter for shipping cost?','Many carriers charge based on whichever is greater: actual weight or volumetric weight (derived from CBM) — bulky, lightweight items often ship at the volumetric rate.',0,'published'),
(90,45,'Should I measure the product or the packed carton?','Always measure the outer packed carton dimensions, since that\'s the actual space it occupies in transit.',1,'published'),
(91,46,'What divisor should I use?','5000 is common for international air freight; some couriers use 6000 for domestic services — always confirm with your specific carrier\'s rate card.',0,'published'),
(92,46,'Why would I be charged more than the actual weight?','If your package is bulky but light, its volumetric weight (based on size) can exceed its actual weight — carriers bill whichever is higher.',1,'published'),
(93,47,'What is \"chargeable weight\"?','The weight your carrier actually bills against — whichever is greater between the shipment\'s actual weight and its volumetric (dimensional) weight.',0,'published'),
(94,47,'What fees should I include as \"additional fees\"?','Customs clearance, fuel surcharges, handling fees, or insurance — any fixed cost not already captured in the per-kg freight rate.',1,'published'),
(95,48,'Is BMI an accurate measure of health?','It\'s a useful population-level screening tool, but it doesn\'t account for muscle mass, bone density, or body composition — always discuss results with a healthcare provider.',0,'published'),
(96,48,'What are the standard BMI categories?','Under 18.5 = underweight, 18.5–24.9 = normal, 25–29.9 = overweight, 30+ = obese, per WHO guidelines.',1,'published'),
(97,49,'Does this split the tip evenly regardless of what each person ordered?','Yes, this tool assumes an even split across all people — for itemized splitting based on individual orders, you\'ll need to calculate portions separately.',0,'published'),
(98,49,'What is a standard tip percentage?','In the US, 15-20% is typical for restaurant service; norms vary significantly in other countries, so check local customs when traveling.',1,'published'),
(99,50,'Can I calculate age at a future date?','Yes — enter any future date in the \"as of\" field to see the projected age at that date.',0,'published'),
(100,50,'Does this account for leap years?','Yes — the calculation uses real calendar month lengths, which correctly handles February in leap years.',1,'published'),
(101,51,'Why is temperature handled differently?','Temperature scales have different zero points (e.g. 0°C ≠ 0°F), so conversion requires an offset formula rather than a simple multiplier like other units.',0,'published'),
(102,51,'How precise are the conversions?','Results are shown to up to 6 decimal places using standard conversion factors — sufficient precision for virtually all everyday and professional use cases.',1,'published'),
(103,52,'Are these real-time exchange rates?','Only if the site administrator has connected a live currency API — otherwise rates reflect the last manual or scheduled update, shown in the \"last updated\" note.',0,'published'),
(104,52,'Why is a currency missing from the list?','Only currencies configured by the site administrator appear here — ask them to add the exchange rate you need under Admin → Settings → Currency API.',1,'published'),
(105,53,'What does a negative percentage change mean?','It indicates the value decreased from X to Y — e.g. -20% means Y is 20% lower than X.',0,'published'),
(106,53,'What\'s the difference between the three modes?','\"X% of Y\" finds a portion of a number; \"X is what % of Y\" finds a ratio; \"% change\" measures growth or decline between two values over time.',1,'published'),
(107,54,'Does the order of the two dates matter?','No — the tool automatically calculates the absolute difference regardless of which date is entered first.',0,'published'),
(108,54,'Is today included in the day count?','The total is the calendar difference between the two exact dates entered; add or subtract one day manually if your use case needs an inclusive count.',1,'published'),
(109,55,'Does this QR code expire?','No — this generates a static QR code that directly encodes your text/URL; it will work indefinitely as long as the destination URL itself is valid.',0,'published'),
(110,55,'Can I scan this with any phone?','Yes — any modern smartphone camera app can scan a standard QR code without needing a separate scanner app.',1,'published'),
(111,56,'Does this handle daily overtime rules?','No — it totals for the week against a single weekly threshold; some jurisdictions also have daily overtime rules which this tool does not check.',0,'published'),
(112,56,'Can I use partial hours?','Yes — enter hours in decimal form, e.g. 7.5 for seven and a half hours.',1,'published'),
(113,57,'What if my balance goes negative?','A negative result means you\'ve requested more leave than you have accrued — check with HR about unpaid leave or carryover policy.',0,'published'),
(114,57,'Does this include sick leave?','Use it for whichever leave bucket you\'re tracking — enter that bucket\'s own accrued/used/pending figures.',1,'published'),
(115,58,'Does this exclude weekends?','No — it adds calendar days. If your contract specifies working days, adjust the days entered accordingly.',0,'published'),
(116,58,'Can I use this for a notice period in weeks?','Yes — multiply the number of weeks by 7 and enter that as days.',1,'published'),
(117,59,'Why is the total so much higher than salary?','Benefits (health insurance, retirement matching) and mandatory payroll taxes typically add 20-40% on top of base salary alone.',0,'published'),
(118,59,'Should I include recruiting costs?','This tool covers ongoing annual costs; one-time recruiting/onboarding costs can be added into \"other overhead\" if you want them reflected.',1,'published'),
(119,60,'What if my supplier\'s lead time varies?','Use their average or worst-case lead time and add a safety buffer to cover typical variability.',0,'published'),
(120,60,'Does this account for customs delays?','Not automatically — add expected customs/clearance time into the safety buffer for international shipments.',1,'published'),
(121,61,'What utilization rate should I use?','65-80% is typical for standard racked storage with aisle access; bulk floor-stacked storage can run higher.',0,'published'),
(122,61,'Does this account for racking height?','Indirectly — your \"volume per unit\" and utilization inputs should reflect your actual racking configuration.',1,'published'),
(123,62,'Does this consider carton rotation?','Yes — it checks both length/width orientations of the carton on the pallet footprint and picks the better fit.',0,'published'),
(124,62,'What is a standard pallet size?','The default 48\"×40\" reflects the standard GMA pallet used widely in North America — adjust for Euro pallets (1200×800mm) or custom sizes.',1,'published'),
(125,63,'Why 85% and not 100%?','Real-world loading always leaves some unusable space for load stability, odd-shaped cargo, and dunnage — 85% is a realistic planning estimate.',0,'published'),
(126,63,'What if volume and weight give different answers?','The container is limited by whichever is lower — light, bulky cargo is usually volume-limited; dense cargo is usually weight-limited.',1,'published'),
(127,64,'Can MOQs be negotiated?','Often yes, especially with an ongoing purchasing relationship or by combining multiple SKUs into one order.',0,'published'),
(128,64,'What if I need less than the MOQ?','Consider splitting the excess with another buyer, holding extra inventory, or sourcing from a supplier with a lower MOQ.',1,'published'),
(129,65,'How is CAGR different from total return?','Total return is the overall % gain over the whole period; CAGR expresses that as a smoothed annual rate, useful for comparing across different time horizons.',0,'published'),
(130,65,'Does this account for additional contributions?','No — this measures growth of a single initial lump sum only.',1,'published'),
(131,66,'Is ROI the same as profit margin?','No — ROI relates profit to investment cost; profit margin relates profit to revenue. They answer different questions.',0,'published'),
(132,66,'Should \"cost\" include ongoing expenses?','Yes — include all costs associated with the investment, not just the initial purchase price, for an accurate ROI.',1,'published'),
(133,67,'How do I calculate two stacked discounts?','Apply the first discount, then apply the second discount to the already-discounted price — do not simply add the two percentages together.',0,'published'),
(134,67,'Does this include sales tax?','No — this calculates the discounted price only; add tax separately based on your local rate.',1,'published'),
(135,68,'Where do I find my effective tax rate?','Divide your total tax paid by your total income on a prior tax return, or check your tax authority\'s published bracket tables for an estimate.',0,'published'),
(136,68,'Does this replace tax software?','No — this is a quick estimate only; use qualified tax software or a professional for actual filing.',1,'published'),
(137,69,'Is tax applied before or after the discount?','This tool applies tax after the discount, matching standard practice in most jurisdictions — confirm your local rules if unsure.',0,'published'),
(138,69,'Can I use this for a quote instead of an invoice?','Yes — the calculation is identical for quotes, estimates, or invoices.',1,'published'),
(139,70,'What if an asset has no salvage value?','Enter 0 — the full cost will be depreciated evenly over the useful life.',0,'published'),
(140,70,'Is straight-line the only depreciation method?','No — declining balance and units-of-production are common alternatives; straight-line is simplest and most widely used for financial statements.',1,'published'),
(141,71,'Why is markup always higher than margin?','Markup divides profit by cost (the smaller number), while margin divides by price (the larger number) — so for the same profit, markup % is always higher.',0,'published'),
(142,71,'Which one should I use for pricing?','Most retailers think in markup when pricing from cost, but margin is more useful for comparing overall profitability against revenue.',1,'published'),
(143,72,'What are standard aging buckets?','Current, 1-30, 31-60, 61-90, and 90+ days overdue are the standard buckets used in most AR aging reports.',0,'published'),
(144,72,'What payment terms should I use?','Use the exact terms stated on the invoice, e.g. Net 30 means 30 days from the invoice date.',1,'published'),
(145,73,'What does \"Net 30\" mean?','Payment is due 30 calendar days from the invoice date — \"Net\" simply refers to the full amount due after that period.',0,'published'),
(146,73,'Should I pay early to get a discount?','If your supplier offers an early-payment discount (e.g. \"2/10 Net 30\" — 2% off if paid within 10 days), it\'s usually worth taking if cash flow allows.',1,'published'),
(147,74,'What does negative working capital mean?','It suggests current liabilities exceed current assets, which can indicate a short-term liquidity risk — though some business models (e.g. subscription businesses) operate normally with negative working capital.',0,'published'),
(148,74,'How is this different from the current ratio?','Working capital is a dollar amount (assets minus liabilities); the current ratio is the same relationship expressed as a ratio (assets divided by liabilities).',1,'published'),
(149,75,'What is considered a good ROE?','15-20% is often considered strong, though this varies significantly by industry — compare against sector peers rather than a universal benchmark.',0,'published'),
(150,75,'Can high ROE be misleading?','Yes — a company with heavy debt can post a high ROE simply from leverage rather than genuinely efficient operations; check debt levels alongside ROE.',1,'published'),
(151,76,'Is a positive variance always good?','No — it depends on context. A positive variance on revenue is good (outperformance), but a positive variance on costs usually means overspending.',0,'published'),
(152,76,'What variance percentage should trigger review?','This varies by organization, but many finance teams flag variances beyond ±5-10% for management explanation.',1,'published'),
(153,77,'What characters need encoding?','Spaces, &, ?, #, %, and other reserved URL characters need encoding when they appear as part of a value rather than URL structure.',0,'published'),
(154,77,'Is this the same as Base64 encoding?','No — URL encoding (percent-encoding) is a different, URL-specific scheme from Base64, which is used for embedding binary data as text.',1,'published'),
(155,78,'Does this verify the token is valid?','No — this only decodes the header and payload for inspection; it does not verify the cryptographic signature, so never trust a decoded token as proof of authenticity.',0,'published'),
(156,78,'Is my token sent to a server?','No — decoding happens entirely in your browser using JavaScript; nothing is transmitted.',1,'published'),
(157,79,'Does this match other languages\' regex syntax exactly?','It uses JavaScript regex syntax, which is very similar to PCRE (used by PHP, Python) but has some differences — verify in your target language for critical patterns.',0,'published'),
(158,79,'Why does my pattern only match once?','Add the \"g\" (global) flag to find all matches rather than just the first one.',1,'published'),
(159,80,'Why is MD5 not available?','MD5 is cryptographically broken (collision attacks are practical) and isn\'t exposed by the browser\'s secure Web Crypto API — use SHA-256 or higher instead.',0,'published'),
(160,80,'Can I reverse a hash back to the original text?','No — hash functions are one-way by design; the only way to \"reverse\" one is to guess inputs and hash them until a match is found.',1,'published'),
(161,81,'Why would I need to encode HTML entities?','To display code samples or user content safely on a webpage without the browser interpreting < and > as actual HTML tags.',0,'published'),
(162,81,'Does this fully prevent XSS?','Entity-encoding untrusted input is one important defense, but full XSS prevention also depends on proper context-aware output escaping in your actual application code.',1,'published'),
(163,82,'What does \"Lorem Ipsum\" mean?','It\'s scrambled Latin from a classical text, used since the 1500s as neutral placeholder text that doesn\'t distract from evaluating layout and typography.',0,'published'),
(164,82,'Is the generated text always different?','Yes — each generation randomly assembles sentences from the Lorem Ipsum word set, so the output varies each time.',1,'published'),
(165,83,'Does this validate my SQL syntax?','No — it only reformats the query for readability; it does not check whether the SQL is syntactically or semantically valid.',0,'published'),
(166,83,'Does this work for all SQL dialects?','The keyword formatting is intentionally generic and works reasonably well across MySQL, PostgreSQL, and SQL Server dialects.',1,'published'),
(167,84,'Is my image uploaded to a server?','No — the conversion happens entirely in your browser using the FileReader API; the image never leaves your device.',0,'published'),
(168,84,'When should I NOT use Base64 image embedding?','For large images or images reused across many pages — Base64 increases size ~33% and prevents the browser from caching the image separately.',1,'published'),
(169,85,'Is my image uploaded anywhere?','No — compression happens entirely in your browser using the HTML5 Canvas API; nothing is sent to a server.',0,'published'),
(170,85,'Does this work on PNG files?','Yes, though PNG is lossless so the quality slider has less visible effect than it does on JPEG images.',1,'published'),
(171,86,'Will resizing distort my image?','Only if you resize without locking the aspect ratio — with it locked, the image scales proportionally with no distortion.',0,'published'),
(172,86,'Is my image uploaded anywhere?','No — resizing happens entirely in your browser using the Canvas API.',1,'published'),
(173,87,'How is this different from a random palette generator?','This generates a systematic tonal scale of ONE color at different lightness levels, rather than several unrelated random colors — better suited to UI design systems.',0,'published'),
(174,87,'Can I use these colors for accessible text?','Check contrast ratios separately — very light or very dark shades in the scale may or may not meet WCAG contrast requirements depending on background.',1,'published'),
(175,88,'Can I use more than two colors?','This tool builds a simple two-color gradient; for multi-color gradients you can extend the generated CSS manually by adding more color-stop values.',0,'published'),
(176,88,'What angle should I use?','135deg (diagonal, top-left to bottom-right) is a very common default for modern UI hero sections.',1,'published'),
(177,89,'What DPI should I use for photo prints?','300 DPI is the standard target for photo-quality prints viewed up close.',0,'published'),
(178,89,'Can I print larger than this result?','Yes, but at a lower effective DPI, which may look pixelated up close — this is fine for large-format prints viewed from a distance.',1,'published'),
(179,90,'Why does pixel size depend on DPI?','Pixels have no fixed physical size — they represent a resolution-dependent unit, so their real-world size only makes sense once you specify a DPI.',0,'published'),
(180,90,'What DPI should I use for web design?','96 DPI is the standard reference used for CSS pixel-to-inch conversions in web design.',1,'published'),
(181,91,'Where do I find a clip\'s frame count?','Most video editing software (Premiere, Final Cut, DaVinci Resolve) displays frame count in the clip properties or timeline.',0,'published'),
(182,91,'Does frame rate affect the actual video quality?','Frame rate affects motion smoothness, not resolution/sharpness — those are independent properties of a video file.',1,'published'),
(183,92,'Why does the channel banner need a \"safe area\"?','YouTube displays the banner differently on TV, desktop, and mobile — the safe area is the zone guaranteed visible across all of them.',0,'published'),
(184,92,'Do these sizes ever change?','Yes — YouTube periodically updates its specifications; check YouTube\'s official Creator help pages for major campaigns.',1,'published'),
(185,93,'Should I leave extra buffer beyond this estimate?','Yes — add 15-20% for project files, renders/exports, and backups beyond just the raw footage estimate.',0,'published'),
(186,93,'How do I find my average file size per video?','Check a representative sample of your existing footage at the same resolution/bitrate you plan to shoot at.',1,'published'),
(187,94,'Why is my actual upload slower than this estimate?','Advertised internet speeds are best-case; real-world speeds are affected by network congestion, Wi-Fi vs. wired connection, and the destination server\'s own limits.',0,'published'),
(188,94,'What is the difference between Mbps and MB/s?','Mbps is megabits per second; MB/s is megabytes per second. There are 8 bits in a byte, so divide Mbps by 8 to get MB/s.',1,'published'),
(189,95,'What is the difference between GPA and CGPA?','GPA typically refers to a single semester or term; CGPA is the cumulative average across every semester completed so far.',0,'published'),
(190,95,'Does a bad early semester permanently hurt my CGPA?','Its influence shrinks as more credit hours accumulate over time, but it never fully disappears from a true cumulative average.',1,'published'),
(191,96,'Is this conversion officially recognized?','No — conversion scales vary by institution and country; use this as a quick estimate only, and get an official conversion from your target school for formal applications.',0,'published'),
(192,96,'Why do different schools use different scales?','Grading philosophies and score distributions vary by country and institution, so there is no single universal percentage-to-GPA formula.',1,'published'),
(193,97,'What if my weights don\'t add up to 100%?','The tool automatically normalizes the weighted sum to a 100% basis, so partial weight sets still produce a valid percentage.',0,'published'),
(194,97,'Can I use this to find what I need on a final exam?','Yes — enter your completed components as usual, then try different scores for the remaining component to see what result you\'d need.',1,'published'),
(195,98,'Does this update automatically?','No — this calculates a snapshot at the moment you click Calculate; use the Countdown Timer tool if you want a live, continuously ticking display.',0,'published'),
(196,98,'What if the date has already passed?','The tool tells you how many days ago it passed instead of a countdown.',1,'published'),
(197,99,'Does this use degrees or radians for trig functions?','Radians — convert degrees to radians (multiply by π/180) before using sin/cos/tan if your input is in degrees.',0,'published'),
(198,99,'Can I use parentheses for order of operations?','Yes — use ( and ) to group expressions exactly as you would on a physical scientific calculator.',1,'published'),
(199,100,'Why multiply by 1.54?','Dry, loose materials (cement, sand, aggregate) take up more volume than the final wet, compacted concrete — 1.54 is the standard conversion factor used in concrete mix design.',0,'published'),
(200,100,'What mix ratio should I use?','1:2:4 is common for general-purpose concrete; higher-strength applications use richer ratios like 1:1:2 — check your structural requirements or local building code.',1,'published'),
(201,101,'Does moisture content affect this calculation?','Yes — wet sand weighs more per unit volume than dry sand (\"bulking\"), so treat the tonnage figure as an approximation and confirm with your supplier.',0,'published'),
(202,101,'Should I order extra sand?','A 5-10% contingency is common practice to cover spillage and measurement variance.',1,'published'),
(203,102,'How deep should driveway gravel be?','3-4 inches is typical for a residential driveway base; heavier traffic areas may need a deeper base layer — check local guidance.',0,'published'),
(204,102,'Does gravel type affect the weight conversion?','Yes — different stone types and sizes have different densities; 1.4 tons/cu yd is a reasonable average estimate, but ask your supplier for their exact material\'s density.',1,'published'),
(205,103,'What does \"#4 bar\" mean?','Rebar sizes are numbered by their diameter in eighths of an inch — a #4 bar is 4/8\" = 1/2\" in diameter.',0,'published'),
(206,103,'Does this include lap splices and waste?','No — this calculates raw material weight for the specified length only; add extra length for laps, waste, and bends per your structural drawings.',1,'published'),
(207,104,'Should I subtract baseboards or trim?','Typically no — only subtract large openings (doors, windows); trim and baseboards are usually painted the same or a coordinating color anyway.',0,'published'),
(208,104,'What if I\'m calculating multiple walls?','Run this calculation once per wall and sum the results, since each wall may have different openings.',1,'published'),
(209,105,'Why does the loose volume differ from bank volume?','Undisturbed (\"bank\") soil is compacted; once excavated, air gaps between loosened particles increase its volume — this \"swell\" is why the same soil needs more truck capacity to haul than its in-ground volume suggests.',0,'published'),
(210,105,'What swell factor should I use?','It varies by soil type — sandy/gravelly soils swell about 15-20%, clay soils can swell 30-40% or more; a geotechnical report is the authoritative source if available.',1,'published'),
(211,106,'How is this different from board feet?','Cubic feet is a general volume unit; board feet is lumber industry\'s standard unit for pricing (1 bd ft = 144 cubic inches) — use the Board Feet Calculator if you need pricing-ready units.',0,'published'),
(212,106,'Does this account for wood shrinkage from drying?','No — this measures the volume at the given dimensions only; green (unseasoned) lumber will shrink somewhat as it dries.',1,'published'),
(213,107,'What waste percentage should I use?','10-15% is typical for straightforward projects; complex cut lists with many small or angled pieces may need 20% or more.',0,'published'),
(214,107,'Why divide instead of just adding the percentage?','Dividing by (1 − waste%) correctly accounts for waste as a share of the total purchased, giving a more accurate gross figure than simply adding the percentage to net.',1,'published'),
(215,108,'Does this include cost of hardware or finish?','No — this covers lumber cost only; add hardware, fasteners, and finishing materials separately.',0,'published'),
(216,108,'Should I price by board foot or linear foot?','Hardwoods are typically priced by board foot; dimensional softwood lumber is often priced per linear foot instead — use whichever matches your supplier\'s pricing.',1,'published'),
(217,109,'Why is my MPG different from the manufacturer\'s rating?','EPA ratings reflect standardized test conditions; real-world MPG varies with driving style, terrain, weather, and vehicle load.',0,'published'),
(218,109,'How often should I track this?','Tracking each fill-up over time helps spot a declining trend, which can signal maintenance issues like underinflated tires or a failing fuel system.',1,'published'),
(219,110,'Should I include vehicle wear and tear?','This tool covers direct trip costs (fuel, tolls, food); many organizations use a standard per-mile rate (e.g. IRS mileage rate) to also account for wear, maintenance and depreciation.',0,'published'),
(220,110,'How do I estimate tolls for an unfamiliar route?','Most mapping/navigation apps display estimated toll costs when you plan the route — use that figure as your \"additional costs\" input.',1,'published'),
(221,111,'Is this the same as driving distance?','No — this is straight-line (\"as the crow flies\") distance. Actual road distance is typically longer and depends on the route taken.',0,'published'),
(222,111,'Where do I find latitude/longitude coordinates?','Most mapping services (Google Maps, etc.) show coordinates when you right-click or long-press a location on the map.',1,'published'),
(223,112,'Does this account for traffic?','Not automatically — build traffic conditions into your \"average speed\" input for a realistic result.',0,'published'),
(224,112,'What if stops take varying amounts of time?','Use an average time per stop, or run separate calculations for different segments of the route with different stop-time assumptions.',1,'published'),
(225,113,'Does this account for daylight saving time?','Yes — it uses each time zone\'s actual current offset for the specific date entered, correctly reflecting daylight saving status at that time.',0,'published'),
(226,113,'Why do some countries not observe daylight saving?','Daylight saving observance is a matter of national/regional policy — this tool respects each zone\'s actual rules rather than assuming they all switch on the same schedule.',1,'published'),
(227,114,'Does this keep running if I close the tab?','No — this is a browser-based timer that only runs while the page is open; it does not send notifications after you close it.',0,'published'),
(228,114,'How accurate is the countdown?','It uses your device\'s system clock, updated every second — accurate to within a second under normal conditions.',1,'published'),
(229,115,'Is this truly random?','It uses the browser\'s Math.random(), which is pseudo-random and suitable for games, sampling, and everyday use — not recommended for cryptographic or high-stakes security purposes.',0,'published'),
(230,115,'Can I generate numbers with duplicates allowed?','Yes — leave \"No duplicates\" unchecked to allow the same number to be drawn more than once.',1,'published'),
(231,116,'What value should I enter for each item?','Annual usage value (annual quantity sold or consumed × unit cost) is standard, but you can use any consistent value metric that reflects an item\'s importance.',0,'published'),
(232,116,'Are the 80/95% cutoffs fixed?','They\'re the standard convention, but some businesses adjust them — the key idea is that a small share of items usually drives most of the value.',1,'published'),
(233,117,'What threshold should I use?','90 days is a common default, but fast-moving retail may use 30-60 days while slow-moving industrial parts may use 180-365 days.',0,'published'),
(234,117,'Does this account for seasonal items?','No — a seasonal item with no sales in its off-season isn\'t necessarily dead stock, so use judgment alongside this check.',1,'published'),
(235,118,'How do I estimate expected weekly demand for a brand-new product?','Compare it to a similar existing product\'s early sales, use pre-order/waitlist signups, or apply a conservative % of a comparable competitor\'s volume.',0,'published'),
(236,118,'What if my trial sells out early?','That\'s a good problem — it signals you can raise the buffer or order quantity for your next trial or full rollout.',1,'published'),
(237,119,'What counts as \"average daily usage\"?','Total units sold or consumed over a recent period (e.g. the last 30 days), divided by the number of days in that period.',0,'published'),
(238,119,'Does this account for incoming purchase orders?','No — this is based on current on-hand stock only, not stock that\'s still in transit or on order.',1,'published'),
(239,120,'What if I don\'t keep a safety stock reserve?','Enter 0 for safety stock and the check will simply compare available stock against the order quantity.',0,'published'),
(240,120,'What does the shortfall number mean?','It\'s how many additional units you\'d need — from new stock, a backorder, or dipping into the safety reserve — to fully cover the order.',1,'published'),
(241,121,'Does the customer need my number saved?','No — that\'s the whole point of a wa.me link. It opens a chat directly without either side needing to save the other\'s number.',0,'published'),
(242,121,'Does this work on both mobile and desktop?','Yes — on mobile it opens the WhatsApp app, and on desktop it opens WhatsApp Web (or the desktop app if installed).',1,'published'),
(243,122,'Can I edit the generated message?','Yes — it\'s a starting point. Copy it and personalize the wording to match your own voice before sending.',0,'published'),
(244,122,'Does this send the message automatically?','No — nothing is sent automatically. It generates the text (and optionally a WhatsApp link) for you to send yourself.',1,'published'),
(245,123,'Will this come across as rude?','The wording is deliberately polite and professional — a clear, friendly reminder is standard business practice, not rude.',0,'published'),
(246,123,'Can I use this for recurring customers?','Yes — it works for one-off or recurring balances; just re-generate with the updated amount each time.',1,'published'),
(247,124,'Can I use this for tiered commission structures?','This calculates a single flat rate — for tiered structures, calculate each tier\'s portion separately and add them together.',0,'published'),
(248,124,'Does this account for tax withholding?','No — this is the gross commission amount before any tax or withholding.',1,'published'),
(249,125,'What counts as a \"working day\"?','Any day you\'re actively selling — exclude weekends/holidays if you don\'t sell on those days.',0,'published'),
(250,125,'What if I\'ve already hit my target?','The tool will show Rs 0/day needed once achieved sales meet or exceed the target.',1,'published'),
(251,126,'What should I include in \"spend\"?','All costs tied to acquiring customers — ad spend, sales salaries/commissions, marketing tools, promotions — for the same period as the customers counted.',0,'published'),
(252,126,'What\'s a \"good\" CAC?','It depends entirely on your margins and Customer Lifetime Value — a good rule of thumb is CAC should be well below CLV, often by 3x or more.',1,'published'),
(253,127,'Should this use revenue or profit?','This version uses revenue for simplicity — for a profit-based CLV, multiply the result by your average profit margin %.',0,'published'),
(254,127,'How do I estimate \"years a customer stays\"?','Look at your historical data — how long, on average, before a customer stops buying from you? If unsure, start conservative.',1,'published'),
(255,128,'Does this include or exclude returns?','Use whichever revenue figure matches your reporting — net revenue (after returns) gives a more accurate ongoing AOV.',0,'published'),
(256,128,'How can I increase my AOV?','Common tactics: bundle offers, \"buy X get a discount on Y\", minimum-order free shipping thresholds, and upselling at checkout.',1,'published'),
(257,129,'What\'s a good conversion rate?','It varies enormously by industry and lead source — the most useful comparison is your own conversion rate over time, not an external benchmark.',0,'published'),
(258,129,'Should I count only qualified leads?','Either is valid — just be consistent about which you use so your conversion rate stays comparable period to period.',1,'published'),
(259,130,'What\'s a good ROAS?','It depends on your profit margin — a common rule of thumb is a ROAS of at least 3-4x for healthy margins, but low-margin businesses may need much higher.',0,'published'),
(260,130,'How is ROAS different from ROI?','ROAS compares revenue to ad spend only; ROI typically factors in the full cost of goods and operations, giving a profit-based view.',1,'published'),
(261,131,'What does a negative result mean?','A negative percentage means sales declined between the two periods — the tool clearly labels this as a decline.',0,'published'),
(262,131,'Can I use this for any time period?','Yes — day-over-day, week-over-week, month-over-month or year-over-year all work the same way.',1,'published'),
(263,132,'Does this calculate compound interest?','No — this applies a simple flat markup on the remaining balance, common for retail installment/credit sale arrangements, not compound interest financing.',0,'published'),
(264,132,'Can the down payment be zero?','Yes — leave it at 0 to calculate installments on the full price.',1,'published'),
(265,133,'What margin should I use for wholesale vs retail?','It varies by industry, but retail margins are commonly 2-3x wholesale margins to account for the extra selling effort and smaller order sizes.',0,'published'),
(266,133,'Does this account for dealer or distributor pricing too?','This covers wholesale and retail — for dealer/distributor tiers, run the same calculation again with your target margin for that tier.',1,'published'),
(267,134,'How much discount should a bundle offer?','Enough to feel like a real deal (commonly 10-25%) while still being profitable — check your margins on each item in the bundle.',0,'published'),
(268,134,'Can I bundle items with different margins?','Yes, but make sure the overall bundle margin still works — a bundle discount can eat more into a low-margin item than expected.',1,'published'),
(269,135,'What rate should I use?','This tool doesn\'t assume a rate — enter the exact rate that applies to your business/product/region; consult your local tax authority to confirm it.',0,'published'),
(270,135,'What\'s the difference between tax-inclusive and tax-exclusive?','Tax-exclusive means tax gets added on top of your entered amount; tax-inclusive means your entered amount already has tax baked in, and the tool works backward to find it.',1,'published'),
(271,136,'Why does the return rate matter so much?','On a refused COD order, you typically still pay the outbound delivery charge and may pay a return charge too — high refusal rates can turn a profitable-looking product into a loss.',0,'published'),
(272,136,'Does this include return shipping cost?','It uses a simplified estimate (cost + outbound delivery lost on returns) — for exact figures, add your courier\'s specific return-shipping fee to the delivery charge.',1,'published'),
(273,137,'Does this include COD handling fees?','Only if you enter a COD fee % and order value — leave both at 0 to calculate pure shipping cost only.',0,'published'),
(274,137,'What if my courier charges by volumetric weight instead?','Use the Volumetric Weight Calculator (in Logistics & Transport Tools) first to find the chargeable weight, then enter that weight here.',1,'published'),
(275,138,'Should I read this word-for-word on calls?','No — use it as a structure to internalize, then speak naturally. A script read too literally can sound robotic.',0,'published'),
(276,138,'Can I use this for warm calls too?','Yes — just adjust the opening line to reference how you know the prospect (referral, previous inquiry, etc).',1,'published'),
(277,139,'Is this pitch meant to be the whole sales conversation?','No — it\'s an opening pitch to spark interest; the real conversation (questions, objections, details) follows after.',0,'published'),
(278,139,'Can I use this in written proposals too?','Yes — it works well as an opening paragraph for a written quote, proposal, or introductory email.',1,'published'),
(279,140,'Will this work for every objection?','It covers the most common objection types — for unique objections specific to your industry, use the closest match as a structural starting point.',0,'published'),
(280,140,'Should I memorize these responses?','Internalize the structure (acknowledge → reframe → ask a question) rather than memorizing exact words, so it sounds natural in conversation.',1,'published');
